window.SOCIETY_CURRICULUM_9 = [{"id":"understandingss9","index":0,"name":"Understanding Social Science","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 1","emoji":"🧭","palette":{"from":"#38bdf8","to":"#0c4a6e"},"lessons":[{"id":"us1","title":"What Is Social Science?","icon":"🔍","kind":"lesson","story":"You've spent years hearing tales of kings, rivers, and continents in class — but what is this mysterious subject really studying? As Grade 9 begins, an old brass compass called the Study Atlas hums to life, ready to reveal the hidden systems running beneath your everyday life. Your first quest: find out what Social Science actually is.","teach":[{"title":"The Systematic Study of Society","body":"In simple words, Social Science is the systematic study of human society. It does not just tell us what happened or where things are located — it also explains why events occur, how people live together, how governments function, and how economies operate.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Not Physics, Not Chemistry","body":"Subjects like Physics, Chemistry, and Biology study the natural world. Social Science instead focuses on society, institutions, cultures, and human interactions — helping us understand not just what happens, but why it happens and how it all connects.","visual":"🧪"},{"title":"Hidden in Your Daily Life","body":"Your house, your food, the roads you travel, the school you attend, and even the electricity you use all depend on systems of governance, economic production, social cooperation, and the natural environment. Even the simplest daily activities rely on Social Science at work.","visual":"🏠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In simple words, what is Social Science?","options":["The systematic study of human society","The study of chemical reactions","The study of planets and stars","The study of plant and animal cells"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter defines Social Science as the systematic study of human society."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which subjects, unlike Social Science, study the natural world according to the chapter?","options":["Physics, Chemistry, and Biology","History and Geography","Political Science and Economics","Sociology and Anthropology"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter contrasts Social Science with Physics, Chemistry, and Biology, which study the natural world."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The electricity you use, generated at distant power stations and delivered through a complex network, is an example of what?","options":["Systems of governance, production, and cooperation at work","A purely natural, unplanned phenomenon","Something unrelated to Social Science","A topic only studied in Grade 6"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter uses electricity supply as an example of how daily life relies on governance, economic production, and social cooperation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Social Science explains not only what happened, but also...","options":["why it happened and how things are interconnected","only where things are located","only when things happened in history","only which people were involved"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter says Social Science explains why events occur and how different aspects of life are interconnected."}]},{"id":"us2","title":"Time, Traditions, and Timeless Questions","icon":"📜","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Atlas archives, scrolls whisper of villages that grew into towns and towns that grew into cities, and of sages who mapped the five elements of the universe. To move forward, you must walk the path of ancient thinkers like Kautilya, tracing how old ideas about the world still shape how we study society today.","teach":[{"title":"From Villages to Cities","body":"Early human communities depended directly on nature for survival. Over time, people learnt to grow crops, domesticate animals, build settlements, develop tools, exchange goods, and organise systems of governance — villages grew into towns, and towns grew into cities.","visual":"🏘️"},{"title":"The Panchamahabhutas","body":"This idea from Indian philosophical traditions describes the natural world as made of five interconnected elements — earth, water, fire, air, and space. It helps explain how environment influences settlement patterns, occupations, architecture, food habits, and health practices.","visual":"🌍"},{"title":"Vasudhaiva Kutumbakam","body":"This idea, meaning 'the world is one family', expresses the interconnectedness of human societies across regions and cultures — a sense of interdependence that lies at the core of what Social Science studies about global interactions.","visual":"🤝"},{"title":"The Arthashastra","body":"Attributed to Kautilya and composed about 2,300 years ago, the Arthashastra examined administration, economic management, taxation, and the duties of rulers toward their people — showing systematic thinking about governance existed long before modern academic disciplines.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"How Social Scientists Work","body":"Social scientists observe people's lives, conduct interviews and surveys, examine documents, and compare different times and places. By gathering information from multiple sources, they build explanations supported by evidence.","visual":"🔬"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does vasudhaiva kutumbakam mean?","options":["The world is one family","Knowledge is power","Time heals all wounds","Nature above all"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter translates vasudhaiva kutumbakam as 'the world is one family'."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Panchamahabhutas concept names five great elements. Which of these is NOT one of them?","options":["Metal (Loha)","Earth (Prithvi)","Water (Apah)","Fire (Agni)"],"answer":0,"explain":"The five elements are earth, water, fire, air, and space — metal is not among them."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Arthashastra, attributed to Kautilya, examined which of these topics?","options":["Administration, economic management, taxation, and duties of rulers","Only farming techniques","Only poetry and drama","Only astronomy"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter says the Arthashastra examined administration, economic management, taxation, and the duties of rulers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How do social scientists gather evidence, according to the chapter?","options":["By observing lives, conducting interviews and surveys, and examining documents","By relying only on personal opinion","By reading only fiction novels","By ignoring the past completely"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states social scientists observe people's lives, conduct interviews and surveys, and examine documents to build evidence-based explanations."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"About how many years ago was the Arthashastra composed, according to the chapter?","answer":2300,"explain":"The chapter states the Arthashastra was composed about 2,300 years ago."}]},{"id":"us3","title":"Four Guides for One Society","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"A drought has struck a village in the Atlas world, and its effects ripple in every direction — dying crops, empty pockets, and families on the move. To solve the puzzle, you must call on four expert guides — Geography, History, Political Science, and Economics — each seeing the crisis through a different lens.","teach":[{"title":"Many Lenses, One Society","body":"A drought affects crops (environment), farmers' incomes (economy), government relief measures (politics), migration to cities (society), and traditional ways of coping (culture). No single field can fully explain society, so Social Science is a group of related disciplines that together give a comprehensive understanding.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Geography","body":"Geography studies the location and distribution of places, objects, and people, and the relationships between human societies and their surroundings. It uses tools such as maps, globes, atlases, and Geographical Information System (GIS) to investigate its questions.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"History","body":"History is the study of the human past. It draws on literary sources (like manuscripts and travelogues), archaeological sources (monuments and artefacts), epigraphic sources (inscriptions on stone or metal), and numismatic sources (coins and currency) to understand social realities of the past.","visual":"📚"},{"title":"Political Science","body":"Political Science is the study of governance — how and why power is distributed, decisions are made, and policies are implemented. In India's villages, the Panchayati Raj system embodies grassroots democracy by giving citizens a voice in local development planning.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Economics","body":"Economics helps us understand how individuals and societies use limited resources to meet their needs — studying how goods and services are produced, exchanged, and distributed, and how consumers, producers, and governments make decisions.","visual":"💰"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Grades 9-10, Social Science primarily draws from how many core disciplines?","options":["Four","Two","Six","Ten"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter names four core disciplines: Geography, History, Political Science, and Economics."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which four disciplines form the core of Social Science in Grades 9-10?","options":["Geography, History, Political Science, and Economics","Physics, Chemistry, Biology, and Maths","Sociology, Psychology, Anthropology, and Philosophy","Art, Music, Sports, and Literature"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states these four disciplines together provide a comprehensive understanding of society."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which discipline studies the location and distribution of places and the relationships between people and their surroundings?","options":["Geography","History","Political Science","Economics"],"answer":0,"explain":"Geography studies where things are located and how places affect one another."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Coins, currency, and medals used as evidence about the past are called what kind of historical source?","options":["Numismatic source","Epigraphic source","Literary source","Archaeological source"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter defines numismatic sources as coins, currency, or medals used to study history."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"India's Panchayati Raj system, which gives citizens a voice in local development, is discussed under which discipline?","options":["Political Science","Geography","Economics","History"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter discusses the Panchayati Raj system as an example within Political Science's study of governance."}]},{"id":"us4","title":"Why It Matters, Now and Tomorrow","icon":"🌱","kind":"lesson","story":"The final gate of this leg of your journey asks not what you know, but what you will do with it. As a citizen of a fast-changing world, you must prove that Social Science is not just facts to memorise — it is a compass for understanding diversity, exercising your rights, and preparing for tomorrow's challenges.","teach":[{"title":"Understanding Diversity and Unity","body":"Social Science explains differences in language, customs, and occupations within the same country as outcomes of geographical, historical, cultural, and economic factors. This understanding builds respect and cooperation, while also helping us see the underlying unity that binds India's diversity together.","visual":"🇮🇳"},{"title":"Active, Informed Citizens","body":"In a democratic society, laws, rights, and responsibilities guide how people live together. Understanding how governments function and how decisions are made prepares you to participate responsibly in civic life and ask informed questions about shared challenges like environmental protection and public health.","visual":"🗳️"},{"title":"Connecting Past, Present, and Future","body":"Social Science connects the past, present, and future. By understanding how past decisions shaped the present, we become better equipped to make wiser choices for the future.","visual":"⌛"},{"title":"The Road Ahead","body":"As new technologies, expanding cities, environmental concerns, and global connections reshape how people live, Social Science will help societies address complex issues such as climate change, sustainable development, and the equitable use of resources.","visual":"🌱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the chapter, understanding differences in language and customs within a country helps build...","options":["respect and cooperation among communities","division and conflict","confusion about identity","disinterest in society"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter says this understanding builds respect and cooperation among communities."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a democratic society, understanding how governments function helps citizens to...","options":["participate responsibly in civic life","avoid all public matters","ignore laws and rights","depend only on officials"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states this understanding prepares citizens to participate responsibly in civic life."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The chapter says Social Science connects which time periods?","options":["Past, present, and future","Only the past","Only the future","Only the present"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states Social Science connects the past, present, and future."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is listed as a challenge Social Science will help societies address in the future?","options":["Climate change and sustainable development","Ancient pottery classification only","Ending all sporting events","None of these"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter lists climate change, sustainable development, social harmony, and equitable use of resources as future challenges."}]},{"id":"us-boss","title":"Trial of the Study Atlas","icon":"⚔️","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Atlas waits a skeptical guardian who insists that society just happens by chance — that there is nothing to study, nothing to explain. Only by proving how history, geography, institutions, resources, and human choices shape every corner of society can you pass through the final gate.","boss":{"name":"The Chance Guardian","taunt":"Society is just chaos and luck — prove me wrong if you dare!"},"teach":[{"title":"Nothing Happens by Chance","body":"Questions like why some regions flood, or why some communities speak different languages, show that society does not function by chance. It is shaped by history, geography, institutions, resources, and human choices — and Social Science helps us explore such questions through observation, evidence, and reasoning.","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is Social Science, in simple words?","options":["The systematic study of human society","The study of outer space","The study of chemical elements","The study of computer programming"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter defines Social Science as the systematic study of human society."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which four disciplines form the core of Social Science in Grades 9-10?","options":["Geography, History, Political Science, and Economics","Biology, Chemistry, Physics, and Maths","Art, Dance, Music, and Drama","Sociology, Psychology, Anthropology, and Philosophy"],"answer":0,"explain":"These four disciplines together provide a comprehensive understanding of society."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does vasudhaiva kutumbakam mean?","options":["The world is one family","Knowledge without limits","Unity through conflict","Nature over society"],"answer":0,"explain":"It expresses the interconnectedness of human societies across regions and cultures."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"About how many years ago was the Arthashastra, attributed to Kautilya, composed?","answer":2300,"explain":"The chapter states it was composed about 2,300 years ago."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Coins and currency used as historical evidence are called what type of source?","options":["Numismatic source","Epigraphic source","Literary source","Oral source"],"answer":0,"explain":"Numismatic sources are coins, currency, or medals used to study history."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the chapter, society does not function by chance — it is shaped by...","options":["history, geography, institutions, resources, and human choices","random luck alone","only geography","only government decisions"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states society is shaped by history, geography, institutions, resources, and human choices."}]}]},{"id":"earthsurface9","index":1,"name":"Shaping of the Earth's Surface","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 2","emoji":"🌋","palette":{"from":"#fb923c","to":"#7c2d12"},"lessons":[{"id":"es1","title":"Journey to the Earth's Core","icon":"🌍","kind":"lesson","story":"Your expedition drone dives beneath the crust into a world of scorching heat and crushing pressure. To survive the journey to the planet's core, you must map every layer of the Earth, from the ground beneath your feet to the fiery heart within.","teach":[{"title":"Three Main Layers","body":"The Earth is made up of three main layers: the crust, the mantle, and the core. The crust is the outermost layer on which we live; below it lies the mantle, which is very thick and hot; and the core is the innermost layer, extremely hot and heavy.","visual":"🌍"},{"title":"The Lithosphere","body":"The crust, along with the upper part of the mantle, forms the lithosphere — the rigid outer layer of the Earth. This lithosphere is broken into the different tectonic plates that make up the Earth's surface.","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"The Asthenosphere","body":"Beneath the lithosphere lies the asthenosphere, a hot, mobile layer of partially molten rock. Because it is semi-molten, it allows the rigid tectonic plates above it to move slowly.","visual":"🔥"},{"title":"Crust Thickness","body":"The crust is much thinner than the layers below it, and its thickness varies. Continental crust is about 30–40 km thick, while oceanic crust is much thinner, only about 5–7 km thick.","visual":"📏"},{"title":"The Core","body":"The core has two parts. The outer core is a fluid layer made mainly of iron and nickel, while the inner core is a solid, hot spinning ball of metal — the densest part of the Earth.","visual":"⚙️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the three main layers of the Earth, from outside to inside?","options":["Crust, mantle, core","Core, mantle, crust","Lithosphere, asthenosphere, core","Crust, asthenosphere, mantle"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Earth is made of the crust (outermost), the mantle, and the core (innermost)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The lithosphere is made up of which two parts?","options":["The crust and the upper mantle","The crust and the outer core","The mantle and the inner core","The asthenosphere and the core"],"answer":0,"explain":"The crust along with the upper part of the mantle forms the rigid lithosphere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which layer lies just beneath the lithosphere and allows tectonic plates to move?","options":["The asthenosphere","The inner core","The outer core","The crust"],"answer":0,"explain":"The asthenosphere is a hot, semi-molten layer beneath the lithosphere that lets plates slide over it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which part of the Earth is described as a solid, hot, spinning metal ball and the densest part of the Earth?","options":["The inner core","The outer core","The mantle","The crust"],"answer":0,"explain":"The inner core is solid, extremely hot, and the densest layer of the Earth."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to the textbook's diagram of the Earth's interior, about how many km thick is the oceanic crust?","answer":5,"explain":"The diagram shows oceanic crust at about 5 km, much thinner than continental crust (about 30 km)."}]},{"id":"es2","title":"Riders on Restless Plates","icon":"🧩","kind":"lesson","story":"The ground beneath you isn't as solid as it seems — you're standing on one of several giant slabs of rock drifting slowly over a sea of molten material. Learn how these tectonic plates move, and which giants rule the planet's surface, before the next great shift begins.","teach":[{"title":"Plate Tectonics Theory","body":"Plate tectonics is an important theory, given by W.J. Morgan, that explains the movement of the Earth's crust. It states that the outermost layer of the Earth is not one single piece but is broken into several large and small tectonic plates.","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"Types of Tectonic Plates","body":"Tectonic plates are massive slabs of solid rock that move very slowly, usually a few centimetres per year. There are three main types: continental plates (which carry continents), oceanic plates (which carry ocean floors), and mixed plates (which carry both continents and oceans).","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"Convection Currents","body":"The movement of tectonic plates is caused by convection currents in the mantle. Heat from the Earth's core causes molten material to rise while cooler material sinks, and this continuous movement pushes and pulls the plates in different directions.","visual":"♨️"},{"title":"Major Tectonic Plates","body":"Some of the major tectonic plates of the world include the Pacific Plate, Eurasian Plate, African Plate, North American Plate, South American Plate, Indo-Australian Plate, and Antarctic Plate.","visual":"🌐"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who gave the theory of plate tectonics?","options":["W.J. Morgan","Charles Darwin","Alfred Wegener alone","Isaac Newton"],"answer":0,"explain":"The text credits the theory of plate tectonics to W.J. Morgan."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What causes tectonic plates to move?","options":["Convection currents in the mantle","Ocean tides","Wind erosion","Gravity from the Moon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rising and sinking material in the mantle creates convection currents that push and pull the plates."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of tectonic plate carries both continents and oceans?","options":["Mixed plate","Continental plate","Oceanic plate","Core plate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mixed plates carry both continents and ocean floor, unlike purely continental or oceanic plates."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is one of the major tectonic plates named in the textbook?","options":["Indo-Australian Plate","Himalayan Plate","Thar Plate","Ganges Plate"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Indo-Australian Plate is listed among the major plates, alongside the Pacific, Eurasian, African, North American, South American, and Antarctic plates."}]},{"id":"es3","title":"Where Giants Collide","icon":"💥","kind":"lesson","story":"Two colossal plates are on a collision course, and where they meet, mountains rise, oceans crack open, and the ground trembles. Master the three types of plate boundaries to predict where the next earthquake or volcano will strike.","teach":[{"title":"Convergent Boundary","body":"At a convergent boundary, two plates move towards each other. When continental plates collide, they form fold mountains, such as the Himalaya; when an oceanic plate collides with a continental plate, the oceanic plate sinks beneath it, leading to volcanic activity and earthquakes.","visual":"🏔️"},{"title":"Divergent Boundary","body":"At a divergent boundary, plates move away from each other. Magma rises from below and forms new crust, creating features such as mid-ocean ridges — the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is a good example.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Transform Boundary","body":"At a transform boundary, plates slide past each other without creating or destroying crust. This type of movement mainly causes earthquakes, such as along the San Andreas Fault in the United States.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"The Ring of Fire","body":"Most earthquakes and volcanoes occur along plate boundaries, especially around the Pacific Ocean, an area known as the Ring of Fire. Plate tectonics helps identify earthquake- and volcano-prone regions and manage disasters arising from them.","visual":"🌋"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What landform results when two continental plates collide, as with the Himalaya?","options":["Fold mountains","Mid-ocean ridges","Sand dunes","Deltas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Colliding continental plates crumple the crust upward to form fold mountains like the Himalaya."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At which type of plate boundary do plates move away from each other, creating new crust?","options":["Divergent boundary","Convergent boundary","Transform boundary","Static boundary"],"answer":0,"explain":"At divergent boundaries, magma rises and forms new crust, such as at the Mid-Atlantic Ridge."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The San Andreas Fault, known for causing earthquakes without creating or destroying crust, is an example of which boundary?","options":["Transform boundary","Convergent boundary","Divergent boundary","Oceanic boundary"],"answer":0,"explain":"At transform boundaries, plates slide past each other, mainly causing earthquakes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the name of the earthquake- and volcano-prone zone that circles the Pacific Ocean?","options":["The Ring of Fire","The Fault Belt","The Volcanic Crown","The Pacific Rift"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most of the world's earthquakes and volcanoes occur around the Pacific Ocean, called the Ring of Fire."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What happens when an oceanic plate collides with a continental plate?","options":["The oceanic plate sinks beneath the continental plate, causing volcanic activity and earthquakes","The continental plate sinks beneath the oceanic plate","Both plates instantly melt","A mid-ocean ridge forms between them"],"answer":0,"explain":"The denser oceanic plate sinks beneath the continental plate, leading to volcanic activity and earthquakes."}]},{"id":"es4","title":"The Slow Siege on Stone","icon":"🪨","kind":"lesson","story":"Wind, rain, frost, and roots are silently attacking every rock you see, breaking it down grain by grain and carrying the pieces far away. Decode the twin forces of weathering and erosion before they reshape the land beneath your boots.","teach":[{"title":"What Is Weathering?","body":"Weathering is the process through which rocks on the Earth's surface break down into smaller pieces. Weathering does not involve movement of the broken material — only the breaking down — and it plays an important role in shaping the Earth's surface and forming soil.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"Three Types of Weathering","body":"Physical weathering breaks rocks into smaller pieces due to temperature changes, frost, or wind. Chemical weathering changes minerals in rocks through reactions with water, air, or acids. Biological weathering is caused by plants, animals, or micro-organisms, such as when plant roots grow into cracks and split rocks apart.","visual":"🌱"},{"title":"What Is Erosion?","body":"Erosion is the process by which soil, rocks, and other surface materials are worn away and carried from one place to another by natural agents like water, wind, ice, or waves. Unlike weathering, erosion involves movement of the broken material.","visual":"🌬️"},{"title":"Types of Erosion","body":"There are several types of erosion: water erosion, caused by rivers, rain, or ocean waves; wind erosion, common in dry and sandy areas; glacial erosion, where moving ice scrapes and carries rocks; and coastal erosion, where sea waves wear away land along the shore.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Erosion and Human Life","body":"Erosion affects many human occupations: it removes fertile topsoil needed by farmers, washes away land, houses, and roads near rivers and coasts, destabilises land in construction and mining, and damages beaches, rivers, and fertile land relied on by tourism and fishing.","visual":"🏚️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the key difference between weathering and erosion?","options":["Weathering breaks down rock in place; erosion also moves the broken material away","Weathering only happens underwater; erosion only happens on land","Weathering is caused by rivers only; erosion is caused by wind only","There is no real difference between them"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weathering breaks rock down without moving it, while erosion carries the broken material away."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Plant roots growing into cracks and splitting a rock apart is an example of which type of weathering?","options":["Biological weathering","Physical weathering","Chemical weathering","Coastal weathering"],"answer":0,"explain":"Biological weathering is caused by plants, animals, or micro-organisms, like roots splitting rock."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Rock changing because of reactions with water, air, or acids is called?","options":["Chemical weathering","Physical weathering","Biological weathering","Glacial erosion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chemical weathering happens when minerals in rocks react with water, air, or acids."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of erosion is caused by moving ice that scrapes and carries rocks?","options":["Glacial erosion","Water erosion","Wind erosion","Coastal erosion"],"answer":0,"explain":"Glacial erosion occurs where moving ice scrapes and carries rocks."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does erosion mainly affect farmers?","options":["It removes the fertile topsoil needed for crop growth, lowering yields","It makes soil more fertile every year","It has no effect on agriculture","It only affects farmers living near deserts"],"answer":0,"explain":"Erosion strips away fertile topsoil that farmers depend on, leading to lower crop yields."}]},{"id":"es5","title":"Down the River's Course","icon":"🏞️","kind":"lesson","story":"A river begins its life as a raging mountain torrent and ends as a slow, sediment-laden giant spilling into the sea. Follow its course from steep waterfalls to looping meanders and fertile deltas to master the art of reading a river.","teach":[{"title":"A River's Three Courses","body":"Rivers shape the land through erosion, transportation, and deposition. In the upper course, steep gradients create V-shaped valleys, waterfalls, and rapids. In the middle course, the river meanders, forming oxbow lakes and floodplains. In the lower course, it deposits large amounts of sediment, forming deltas, levees, and alluvial fans.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"Waterfalls","body":"A waterfall forms where a river flows over a steep cliff or vertical drop, usually in the upper course, where hard rocks resist erosion while softer rocks below are worn away, creating a sudden drop. Waterfalls are also used for hydroelectric power generation and attract tourism.","visual":"💦"},{"title":"Meanders","body":"A meander is a winding curve in the middle or lower course of a river, formed by lateral erosion and deposition of sediments. The river erodes the outer banks of bends and deposits sediment on the inner banks, gradually creating large loops that support agriculture and settlement.","visual":"🌀"},{"title":"Deltas","body":"A delta is a landform formed at the mouth of a river, where it flows into a sea, ocean, or lake and deposits the sediment it has carried. Over time, deposits accumulate into a fan-shaped or triangular area of highly fertile, alluvial land, ideal for crops like rice and jute.","visual":"🔺"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which part of a river's course do waterfalls, V-shaped valleys, and rapids typically form?","options":["The upper course","The middle course","The lower course","The delta"],"answer":0,"explain":"Steep gradients and strong erosive forces in the upper course create waterfalls, rapids, and V-shaped valleys."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a winding curve or bend in the middle or lower course of a river called?","options":["A meander","A delta","A moraine","A fjord"],"answer":0,"explain":"A meander is a winding bend formed by lateral erosion and deposition."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is a delta?","options":["A fan-shaped landform formed at a river's mouth by deposited sediment","A steep drop where a river flows over a cliff","A bowl-shaped depression at the head of a glacier","A ridge of sand formed by wind"],"answer":0,"explain":"A delta forms where a river meets the sea, ocean, or lake and deposits its sediment load."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a meander, where does the river mainly deposit sediment?","options":["On the inner bank of the bend","On the outer bank of the bend","Only at the river's source","Only underground"],"answer":0,"explain":"The river erodes the outer bank while depositing sediment on the inner bank, forming loops."}]},{"id":"es6","title":"Sculptors of Coast, Ice, Wind, and Stone","icon":"🏔️","kind":"lesson","story":"From wave-battered sea stacks to ice-carved fjords, wind-sculpted dunes, and dripping limestone caves, the Earth's surface is a gallery carved by water, ice, wind, and hidden underground rivers. Explore every corner of this gallery to become a true landform expert.","teach":[{"title":"Coastal Landforms","body":"Waves and currents constantly reshape coastal land, creating landforms such as beaches, sea cliffs, wave-cut platforms, sea caves, arches, and stacks. An arch forms when caves on opposite sides of a headland meet; a stack is an isolated pillar of rock left standing after an arch collapses.","visual":"🏖️"},{"title":"Glacial Landforms","body":"Glacial erosion carves U-shaped valleys, cirques (bowl-shaped depressions at the head of a glacier), aretes (sharp ridges between valleys), hanging valleys, and fjords (deep, narrow inlets formed when the sea floods glacial valleys).","visual":"🧊"},{"title":"Moraines","body":"Moraines are landforms created by the deposition of rocks, soil, and debris (called till) carried and left behind by glaciers. Lateral moraines form along the sides of glaciers, terminal moraines mark a glacier's furthest advance, and medial moraines form where two glaciers meet and their lateral moraines join.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"Wind Landforms","body":"Wind erosion creates yardangs (streamlined rock ridges), ventifacts (rocks polished by sandblasting), deflation hollows, and desert pavements. Wind also builds dunes, including crescent-shaped barchan dunes, ridge-like longitudinal dunes, multi-armed star dunes, and vegetation-stabilised parabolic dunes.","visual":"🏜️"},{"title":"Underground Water and Karst","body":"In limestone regions, underground water creates Karst topography through chemical weathering and erosion, including caves, stalactites (hanging from the cave ceiling), stalagmites (rising from the cave floor), sinkholes (dolines), and underground rivers.","visual":"🕳️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What coastal landform is created when caves on opposite sides of a headland meet?","options":["A sea arch","A sea stack","A wave-cut platform","A beach"],"answer":0,"explain":"A sea arch forms when erosion connects caves from both sides of a headland."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which glacial landform is a deep, narrow inlet formed when the sea floods a glacial valley?","options":["A fjord","A cirque","An arete","A moraine"],"answer":0,"explain":"A fjord is created when the sea floods a glacially carved valley."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Lateral, terminal, and medial are all types of which glacial landform?","options":["Moraines","Fjords","Dunes","Yardangs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Moraines are deposits of till left by glaciers, classed as lateral, terminal, or medial."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A crescent-shaped sand dune that forms where sand is limited and wind blows from one direction is called a?","options":["Barchan dune","Star dune","Longitudinal dune","Parabolic dune"],"answer":0,"explain":"Barchan dunes are crescent-shaped and form with limited sand and a single wind direction."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the difference between a stalactite and a stalagmite in a cave?","options":["A stalactite hangs from the ceiling; a stalagmite rises from the floor","A stalactite rises from the floor; a stalagmite hangs from the ceiling","Both hang from the ceiling","Both rise from the floor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Stalactites are icicle-shaped formations hanging from cave ceilings, while stalagmites rise from the floor."}]},{"id":"es7","title":"When the Land Turns Deadly","icon":"⚠️","kind":"lesson","story":"The same landforms that shape our world can turn dangerous without warning — a slope gives way, a glacial lake bursts its banks, or a wall of snow comes crashing down. Study the causes of these disasters so you can help protect lives when the ground turns against you.","teach":[{"title":"Landslides","body":"Landslides are caused by natural and human factors that make slopes unstable. Heavy rainfall, earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, steep slopes, and loose weathered rock are natural causes, while deforestation, mining, road construction, and unplanned building on hillsides are human causes.","visual":"⛰️"},{"title":"Avalanches","body":"Avalanches are caused by the sudden instability of snow on steep mountain slopes. Heavy snowfall, weak or loosely bonded snow layers, a sudden rise in temperature, and strong winds can all destabilise the snowpack, while earthquakes and human activities like skiing or construction can trigger a collapse.","visual":"🏔️"},{"title":"GLOFs","body":"Glacial Lake Outburst Floods (GLOFs) happen when large volumes of water are suddenly released from glacial lakes. Rapid glacier melting increases pressure on natural dams made of ice or loose moraines, and earthquakes, avalanches, or landslides can cause these dams to collapse, releasing destructive floods downstream.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Dust Storms","body":"Dust storms occur when strong winds lift large amounts of loose, dry soil and sand into the air. They are common in desert and semi-arid regions where prolonged drought, sparse vegetation from deforestation or overgrazing, and climate change leave the land exposed.","visual":"🌪️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does GLOF stand for?","options":["Glacial Lake Outburst Flood","Global Landform Outflow Force","Ground Level Ocean Flood","Glacier Land Overflow Factor"],"answer":0,"explain":"A GLOF is a Glacial Lake Outburst Flood, caused by the sudden release of water from a glacial lake."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which disaster is caused by the sudden instability of snow on steep mountain slopes?","options":["An avalanche","A landslide","A dust storm","A GLOF"],"answer":0,"explain":"Avalanches occur when unstable snow on steep slopes suddenly gives way."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which human activity is mentioned in the textbook as a cause of landslides?","options":["Deforestation","Afforestation","Crop rotation","Fishing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Deforestation, along with mining and construction, disturbs slopes and can trigger landslides."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Dust storms are most common in which type of regions?","options":["Desert and semi-arid regions","Tropical rainforests","Polar ice caps","River deltas"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dust storms occur where soil is loose and dry, typical of desert and semi-arid regions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What puts pressure on the natural dams of glacial lakes and can lead to a GLOF?","options":["Rapid melting of glaciers due to rising temperatures","Falling temperatures freezing the lake solid","A decrease in rainfall","Reduced human activity in the mountains"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rapid glacier melting increases the size and water level of glacial lakes, pressuring their natural dams."}]},{"id":"es-boss","title":"Boss: The Tectonic Titan","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"The Tectonic Titan has risen, forged from every layer, plate, and landform you have studied. Prove your mastery of the Earth's surface — one wrong answer and the ground will shift beneath you.","boss":{"name":"The Tectonic Titan","taunt":"You call that geology? I reshaped continents while you were still reading Chapter 1!"},"teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who gave the theory of plate tectonics?","options":["W.J. Morgan","Charles Darwin","Isaac Newton","Alfred Wegener alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"The theory of plate tectonics is credited to W.J. Morgan."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the three types of plate boundaries?","options":["Convergent, divergent, and transform","Continental, oceanic, and mixed","Lithosphere, asthenosphere, and mesosphere","Fold, fault, and fracture"],"answer":0,"explain":"Plates meet at convergent, divergent, or transform boundaries."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which mountain range formed from the collision of continental plates?","options":["The Himalaya","The Andes formed by mid-ocean ridges","The Rockies from a transform fault","The Alps from wind erosion"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Himalaya formed as fold mountains from the collision of continental plates."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main difference between weathering and erosion?","options":["Weathering breaks down rock in place; erosion moves the broken material away","Weathering only occurs underwater","Erosion only breaks rock without moving it","They are two names for the same process"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weathering breaks rock down without moving it, while erosion transports the broken material."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which landform forms at a river's mouth where it deposits sediment into a sea, ocean, or lake?","options":["A delta","A cirque","A yardang","An arete"],"answer":0,"explain":"A delta is the fan-shaped landform built from sediment deposited at a river's mouth."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What natural hazard involves the sudden release of water from a glacial lake when its ice or moraine dam collapses?","options":["A GLOF (Glacial Lake Outburst Flood)","A dust storm","An avalanche","A tsunami"],"answer":0,"explain":"A GLOF occurs when a glacial lake's natural dam suddenly collapses, releasing floodwater downstream."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to the textbook's diagram of the Earth's interior, about how many km thick is the inner core?","answer":1250,"explain":"The diagram (Fig. 2.1) shows the inner core as about 1250 km thick, the densest layer of the Earth."}]}]},{"id":"atmosphere9","index":2,"name":"Atmosphere and Climate","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 3","emoji":"☁️","palette":{"from":"#38bdf8","to":"#0c4a6e"},"lessons":[{"id":"at1","title":"The Air Blanket","icon":"🌫️","kind":"lesson","story":"You step outside and the wind tugs at your sleeve — the sky above you isn't empty, it's a living ocean of gas called the atmosphere. Before you can master storms and monsoons, you must know exactly what this invisible blanket is made of.","teach":[{"title":"What is the Atmosphere?","body":"The atmosphere is a blanket of air surrounding the Earth, held in place by gravity. It is a mixture of gases that shields us from harmful solar radiation, including ultraviolet rays, and regulates Earth's temperature by trapping some of the Sun's energy.","visual":"🌍"},{"title":"Nitrogen and Oxygen Rule the Sky","body":"Nitrogen makes up about 78% of the atmosphere and oxygen about 21%, together making them the two primary and most abundant gases essential to life on Earth.","visual":"💨"},{"title":"The Supporting Cast of Gases","body":"Argon makes up about 0.93%, carbon dioxide about 0.04%, and other gases like helium, neon, krypton, xenon, ozone, and hydrogen make up roughly 0.03%. These are found in much smaller quantities than nitrogen and oxygen.","visual":"⚗️"},{"title":"Water Vapour and Dust","body":"Besides gases, the atmosphere holds water vapour, usually ranging from 0.1% to 0.4%, along with tiny dust particles. Water vapour plays a significant role in cloud formation and precipitation, and the overall composition of the atmosphere varies with altitude.","visual":"💧"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What force holds the atmosphere around the Earth?","options":["Gravity","Magnetism","Air pressure","Wind"],"answer":0,"explain":"Gravity is the force of attraction that pulls the atmosphere down around the Earth."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Roughly what percentage of the atmosphere is nitrogen?","answer":78,"explain":"Nitrogen makes up about 78% of the atmosphere, the most abundant gas present."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which two gases are the primary and most abundant gases in the atmosphere?","options":["Nitrogen and oxygen","Carbon dioxide and argon","Oxygen and ozone","Nitrogen and helium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%) together make up nearly all of the atmosphere and are essential to life."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which atmospheric component plays a significant role in cloud formation and precipitation?","options":["Water vapour","Argon","Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen"],"answer":0,"explain":"Water vapour, generally 0.1% to 0.4% of the atmosphere, is key to cloud formation and precipitation."}]},{"id":"at2","title":"Climbing the Sky Ladder","icon":"🪜","kind":"lesson","story":"A brave aviator asks you to chart a course from the ground to the edge of space. Each layer of sky behaves differently — some hide the weather, some guard us from the Sun, and one even paints the night with colour. Climb carefully.","teach":[{"title":"Troposphere: Where Weather Lives","body":"The troposphere is the most important layer, averaging about 12 kilometres high. Temperature decreases with increasing altitude here, and nearly all weather phenomena — rainfall, fog, and hail — occur in this layer. It ends at the tropopause.","visual":"🌧️"},{"title":"Stratosphere: The Ozone Shield","body":"The stratosphere extends up to about 50 kilometres. It is free of clouds and weather disturbances, making it ideal for flying aeroplanes. It contains the ozone layer, which filters out the Sun's harmful ultraviolet radiation. The stratopause marks its upper boundary.","visual":"✈️"},{"title":"Mesosphere: Meteor Burner","body":"The mesosphere extends up to about 80 kilometres. Like the troposphere, temperature here decreases with increasing altitude, and most meteorites entering from space burn up in this layer.","visual":"☄️"},{"title":"Thermosphere: The Fiery Heights","body":"The thermosphere stretches from 80 to 700 km. Temperature rises very rapidly here as gas molecules absorb X-rays and short-wave ultraviolet radiation. It contains the ionosphere, reflects radio waves back to Earth, and hosts the colourful auroras.","visual":"🌌"},{"title":"Exosphere: The Final Frontier","body":"The exosphere is the uppermost, outermost layer, with very thin air. Light gases like helium and hydrogen escape into space from here due to weak gravity.","visual":"🚀"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which layer of the atmosphere do nearly all weather phenomena, like rainfall and fog, occur?","options":["Troposphere","Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Thermosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The troposphere is where the air we breathe, water vapour, and clouds exist, so weather happens here."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the average height of the troposphere, in kilometres?","answer":12,"explain":"The troposphere has an average height of about 12 kilometres."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which layer contains the ozone layer that filters ultraviolet radiation?","options":["Stratosphere","Troposphere","Mesosphere","Exosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stratosphere contains the ozone layer, which shields us from the Sun's harmful ultraviolet radiation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Most meteorites burn up while passing through which layer?","options":["Mesosphere","Thermosphere","Troposphere","Exosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most meteorites entering from space burn up in the mesosphere."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which layer does temperature rise very rapidly with increasing altitude, and where do auroras occur?","options":["Thermosphere","Stratosphere","Mesosphere","Troposphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the thermosphere, gas molecules absorb X-rays and UV radiation, causing temperature to rise rapidly; auroras also occur here."}]},{"id":"at3","title":"Weather's Daily Mood","icon":"🌦️","kind":"lesson","story":"Will it rain today, or will the sun blaze down? A village elder asks you to explain why the sky keeps changing its mind day to day, yet somehow settles into familiar patterns over the years. Time to decode weather and climate.","teach":[{"title":"Weather vs Climate","body":"Weather refers to the hour-to-hour and day-to-day conditions of the atmosphere, and can vary significantly from day to day. Climate is the average weather conditions of a place over a longer period of time, usually thirty years or more.","visual":"📅"},{"title":"Temperature and Insolation","body":"Temperature varies between day and night and across seasons. Insolation — the incoming solar energy intercepted by the Earth — decreases from the equator towards the poles, so temperature also decreases from the equator towards the poles.","visual":"☀️"},{"title":"Humidity and Precipitation","body":"Humidity is the presence of water vapour in the air; warmer air can hold more water vapour, raising humidity. Precipitation occurs when saturated water vapour condenses and falls to Earth as drizzle, rain, snow, sleet, or hail — rain is the most common form.","visual":"💦"},{"title":"Pressure and Wind","body":"Air pressure is highest at sea level and decreases with altitude. Warm air rises to create low pressure (cloudy, wet weather), while cold, heavy air sinks to create high pressure (clear, sunny skies). Wind is air moving from high-pressure to low-pressure areas — sea breezes blow from sea to land by day, land breezes from land to sea by night.","visual":"🍃"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What term describes the hour-to-hour and day-to-day conditions of the atmosphere?","options":["Weather","Climate","Insolation","Humidity"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weather refers to the short-term, day-to-day conditions of the atmosphere, which can change quickly."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Climate is generally described as the average weather of a place over how long a period?","options":["Thirty years or more","One week","One season","A single year"],"answer":0,"explain":"Climate refers to the sum total of weather conditions over an extended period, usually thirty years or more."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A low-pressure area, formed when warm air rises, is typically associated with what kind of weather?","options":["Cloudy skies and wet weather","Clear and sunny skies","No wind at all","Freezing temperatures only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Low pressure areas form where warm air rises, and are associated with cloudy skies and wet weather."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A local wind that blows from the sea towards the land during the day is called a:","options":["Sea breeze","Land breeze","Monsoon","Westerly"],"answer":0,"explain":"A sea breeze blows from sea to land during the day, especially in the afternoon, when land becomes warmer than the sea."}]},{"id":"at4","title":"The Four Seasons of India","icon":"🍂","kind":"lesson","story":"A farmer hands you an old almanac and asks you to explain when to sow and when to harvest. India's calendar of seasons — official and traditional — holds the answer. Learn the rhythm the whole subcontinent lives by.","teach":[{"title":"India's Tropical Monsoon Climate","body":"The climate of India can be broadly classified as tropical monsoon. The Indian Meteorological Department (IMD) has recognised four distinct seasons in India: winter, summer (pre-monsoon), monsoon (rainy), and post-monsoon.","visual":"🇮🇳"},{"title":"Winter and Summer","body":"Winter generally lasts from December to early April, with December and January the coldest months. Summer or pre-monsoon spans April to June (or July in the north-west), with average temperatures across inland India ranging from 32-40°C.","visual":"❄️"},{"title":"Monsoon and Post-Monsoon","body":"The monsoon or rainy season generally spans June to September, dominated by the humid south-west summer monsoon. The post-monsoon season lasts from October to December, when north-western India is usually cloudless. Himalayan states additionally experience autumn and spring.","visual":"🌧️"},{"title":"The Traditional Six Ritus","body":"Traditionally, India recognises six seasons (Ṛtu), each about two months long: Vasanta, Grīṣhma, Varṣhā, Śharad, Hemanta, and Śhiśhira, based on the astronomical division of the twelve months into six parts.","visual":"📜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many distinct seasons does the Indian Meteorological Department (IMD) officially recognise in India?","answer":4,"explain":"The IMD recognises four seasons: winter, summer/pre-monsoon, monsoon/rainy, and post-monsoon."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which are the coldest months of the year in India?","options":["December and January","March and April","June and July","September and October"],"answer":0,"explain":"December and January are the coldest months, with average north-west temperatures around 10-15°C."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The monsoon or rainy season in India generally spans which months?","options":["June to September","October to December","January to March","April to June"],"answer":0,"explain":"The monsoon or rainy season generally spans June to September, dominated by the south-west summer monsoon."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many traditional seasons (Ṛtu) does the Indian calendar traditionally divide the year into?","answer":6,"explain":"Traditionally, India recognises six seasons, each about two months long, based on dividing the twelve months into six parts."}]},{"id":"at5","title":"Winds That Reverse","icon":"🌬️","kind":"lesson","story":"Ancient sailors once rode these winds across open seas, discovering that they mysteriously reverse direction with the seasons. The Arabs named this phenomenon 'monsoon'. Trace its path to understand why it rules the fate of an entire subcontinent.","teach":[{"title":"What is a Monsoon?","body":"Monsoon refers to the seasonal reversal in wind direction during a year. Ancient sailors first noticed this phenomenon, and Arab traders named it 'monsoon', derived from the Arabic word mausim, meaning season.","visual":"⛵"},{"title":"The South-West (Summer) Monsoon","body":"The south-west monsoon blows from sea to land across the Indian Ocean, Arabian Sea, and Bay of Bengal between June and September. During summer, India's landmass heats faster than the ocean, creating low pressure over land and high pressure over the sea, so moist winds blow inland, bringing most of the country's rainfall.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"The North-East (Winter) Monsoon","body":"The north-east monsoon occurs from October to February. The Indian landmass cools faster than the oceans, creating high pressure over land and low pressure over the sea, so cold, dry winds blow from land to sea. These generally bring little rain, except when they cross the Bay of Bengal and pick up moisture, causing rainfall in Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, and parts of Karnataka.","visual":"🍃"},{"title":"Why Monsoon Matters","body":"Most of India's agriculture depends on monsoon rainfall for sowing and growing crops. Monsoon also affects rivers, reservoirs, daily life, transport, festivals, and employment. However, excessive rainfall can cause floods, while weak monsoons can lead to droughts.","visual":"🌾"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The word 'monsoon' comes from which Arabic word, meaning season?","options":["Mausim","Sahara","Qamar","Rihla"],"answer":0,"explain":"Arab traders named the seasonal wind reversal 'monsoon', derived from the Arabic word mausim, meaning season."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The south-west monsoon, which brings most of India's rainfall, blows during which months?","options":["June to September","October to February","March to May","December to January"],"answer":0,"explain":"The south-west summer monsoon blows from June to September, bringing most of the country's rainfall."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What primarily causes the south-west monsoon winds to blow from the ocean towards India?","options":["Unequal heating of land and sea","The Earth's magnetic field","Volcanic eruptions","Ocean tides"],"answer":0,"explain":"In summer, India's landmass heats faster than the ocean, creating a low-pressure area over land that draws in moist winds from the sea."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The north-east monsoon brings rainfall to Tamil Nadu, Andhra Pradesh, and parts of Karnataka after picking up moisture over which sea?","options":["Bay of Bengal","Arabian Sea","Red Sea","Caspian Sea"],"answer":0,"explain":"When north-east monsoon winds pass over the Bay of Bengal, they pick up moisture and bring rain to India's eastern coast."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main reason monsoon is so important to life in India?","options":["Most agriculture depends on monsoon rainfall","It only affects coastal fishing","It has no effect on daily life","It only matters for tourism"],"answer":0,"explain":"Most of India's agriculture depends on monsoon rainfall, and it also affects water supply, transport, festivals, and employment."}]},{"id":"at6","title":"A Warming World, A Flooding Punjab","icon":"🌏","kind":"lesson","story":"In 2025, swollen rivers burst their banks across Punjab, sweeping away homes and fields. Was it just heavy rain, or something bigger at work? Investigate climate change, your own carbon footprint, and the real disaster that struck close to home.","teach":[{"title":"What is Climate Change?","body":"Climate change refers to long-term changes in weather patterns — temperature, rainfall, and wind — caused mainly by human activities such as burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial pollution that releases gases like carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Greenhouse Gases and Their Effects","body":"Human activities increase greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide, methane, nitrous oxide, and water vapour, which trap heat and raise global temperatures. This leads to more frequent floods, droughts, melting glaciers, rising sea levels, and loss of biodiversity.","visual":"🌋"},{"title":"Your Carbon Footprint","body":"A carbon footprint is the total amount of greenhouse gases released into the atmosphere from human activities like energy use, transportation, or making goods and services. It can be reduced by using renewable energy, protecting forests, and adopting sustainable lifestyles.","visual":"👣"},{"title":"Case Study: Punjab Floods, 2025","body":"In 2025, Punjab suffered severe floods as heavy monsoon rains and western disturbances caused the Satluj, Beas, Ravi, and Ghaggar rivers to overflow. Human-made factors worsened the damage: weak old river embankments (dhūsī bāndh), homes built too close to rivers, silt-clogged riverbeds, and poorly communicated flood warnings.","visual":"🌊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Climate change is caused mainly by which human activities?","options":["Burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial pollution","Ocean currents alone","Natural volcanic activity alone","The rotation of the Earth"],"answer":0,"explain":"Climate change is driven mainly by human activities like burning fossil fuels, deforestation, and industrial pollution."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is an example of a greenhouse gas that traps heat in the atmosphere?","options":["Carbon dioxide","Nitrogen","Argon","Neon"],"answer":0,"explain":"Carbon dioxide, along with methane, nitrous oxide, and water vapour, is a greenhouse gas that traps heat."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What term describes the total greenhouse gases released into the atmosphere due to a person's activities?","options":["Carbon footprint","Insolation","Precipitation","Monsoon index"],"answer":0,"explain":"A carbon footprint is the total amount of greenhouse gases released as a result of human activities like energy use and transport."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which rivers overflowed and caused the severe Punjab floods of 2025?","options":["Satluj, Beas, and Ravi","Ganga, Yamuna, and Kaveri","Indus, Chenab, and Jhelum only","Godavari and Krishna"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Satluj, Beas, Ravi, and Ghaggar rivers, already flowing high, overflowed after heavy monsoon rains, flooding large parts of Punjab."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these was a human-made factor that worsened the Punjab floods of 2025?","options":["Weak and old river embankments (dhūsī bāndh)","Too much forest cover near rivers","Excess groundwater storage","Overly accurate flood warnings"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weak, old river embankments could not hold back the rising water, and were worsened by encroachment near rivers and silt buildup."}]},{"id":"at-boss","title":"The Sky Sovereign's Challenge","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the edge of the exosphere, the Sky Sovereign — ruler of every layer, wind, and season you've studied — descends to test whether you truly understand the atmosphere and India's climate. Answer wisely, or be swept back down to the troposphere.","boss":{"name":"The Sky Sovereign","taunt":"You've breathed my air and ridden my winds — now prove you understand them, or be grounded forever!"},"teach":[{"title":"Final Review: The Atmosphere","body":"Remember: nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%) dominate the atmosphere, five layers rise from troposphere to exosphere, weather is short-term while climate is long-term, and India's tropical monsoon climate is shaped by the seasonal reversal of winds.","visual":"🌍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which two gases together make up about 99% of the Earth's atmosphere?","options":["Nitrogen and oxygen","Carbon dioxide and argon","Oxygen and ozone","Hydrogen and helium"],"answer":0,"explain":"Nitrogen (78%) and oxygen (21%) are the two primary and most abundant gases in the atmosphere."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the average height, in kilometres, of the troposphere — the layer where weather occurs?","answer":12,"explain":"The troposphere has an average height of about 12 kilometres and is where nearly all weather phenomena occur."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which atmospheric layer contains the ozone layer and is ideal for flying aeroplanes?","options":["Stratosphere","Troposphere","Mesosphere","Exosphere"],"answer":0,"explain":"The stratosphere is free of weather disturbances and contains the ozone layer that filters ultraviolet radiation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How is climate best distinguished from weather?","options":["Climate is the average weather of a place over 30+ years; weather is day-to-day conditions","Climate and weather mean exactly the same thing","Weather only refers to temperature; climate refers to wind","Climate changes hourly while weather stays constant for years"],"answer":0,"explain":"Weather is the hour-to-hour and day-to-day state of the atmosphere, while climate is the average pattern over an extended period, usually 30+ years."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many distinct seasons does the Indian Meteorological Department (IMD) officially recognise in India?","answer":4,"explain":"The IMD recognises four seasons: winter, summer/pre-monsoon, monsoon/rainy, and post-monsoon."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The word 'monsoon' is derived from which Arabic word, meaning season?","options":["Mausim","Sahara","Qamar","Rihla"],"answer":0,"explain":"Arab traders named the seasonal wind reversal 'monsoon', derived from the Arabic word mausim, meaning season."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What mainly causes the south-west summer monsoon to blow from the ocean towards India?","options":["Unequal heating of land and sea creating a low-pressure area over land","Cold ocean currents pushing air inland","The Earth's magnetic field","Volcanic activity in the Himalayas"],"answer":0,"explain":"India's landmass heats faster than the ocean in summer, creating low pressure over land that draws in moist winds from the sea."}]}]},{"id":"earlyhumans9","index":3,"name":"Early Humans and Civilisation","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 4","emoji":"🦴","palette":{"from":"#a78bfa","to":"#4c1d95"},"lessons":[{"id":"eh1","title":"Before Writing: Studying Early Humans","icon":"📜","kind":"lesson","story":"You step through a crack in time to an age before a single word was ever written down. To survive here, you'll need to think like an archaeologist -- reading bones and stones instead of books.","teach":[{"title":"Before and After Writing","body":"More than 99 per cent of human history (from about 3 million years ago to 5000 years ago) falls 'before' writing, and is understood mainly through tools, fossils and other material remains. Less than 1 per cent -- the last 5000 years -- falls 'after' writing, when written documents also became a source of evidence.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"Biological Evolution","body":"Biological evolution refers to the gradual physical and genetic changes through which our early ancestors, the australopithecines (australis means southern, pithecus means primates), evolved into modern human beings, Homo sapiens.","visual":"🧬"},{"title":"Cultural Evolution","body":"Cultural evolution explains how humans adapted to their surroundings during the Quaternary Period, the last 26 lakh years. To survive changing climate and environment, humans developed tools, techniques and other technology.","visual":"🛠️"},{"title":"Hominins and the Old World","body":"Hominins are the group that includes modern humans and our early human-like ancestors -- also called 'tool makers', since one of them made the earliest stone tools around 3.3 million years ago. The earliest human settlements of Africa, Asia and Europe are together called the 'Old World'.","visual":"🦴"},{"title":"Out of Africa","body":"Homo erectus, armed with stone hand axes and cleavers, was the first hominin to leave Africa, dispersing into Asia and Europe between about 2 million and 500,000 years ago. Homo sapiens themselves evolved in Africa around 300,000 years ago, with a major migration out of Africa around 125,000 years ago.","visual":"🌍"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the term for the gradual physical and genetic changes through which early ancestors evolved into modern humans?","options":["Biological evolution","Cultural evolution","The Neolithic Revolution","Cuneiform writing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Biological evolution describes the physical and genetic change from australopithecines to Homo sapiens."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which hominin was the first to migrate out of Africa, carrying tools like hand axes and cleavers?","options":["Homo erectus","Homo habilis","Homo sapiens","Homo neanderthalensis"],"answer":0,"explain":"Homo erectus was the first hominin to exit Africa, spreading into Asia and Europe."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to the chapter, about how many thousand years ago did Homo sapiens evolve in Africa?","answer":300,"explain":"The text states Homo sapiens evolved in Africa around 300,000 years ago."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What share of human history falls in the period 'before' writing, according to the chapter's comparison?","options":["More than 99 per cent","Less than 1 per cent","Exactly 50 per cent","About 75 per cent"],"answer":0,"explain":"The 'before writing' period covers more than 99 per cent of human history."}]},{"id":"eh2","title":"Fossils, Tools and Our Ancestors","icon":"🪨","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Olduvai Gorge, half-buried skulls and chipped stones wait for you to brush away the dust. Every fossil and flake you uncover is a clue in the long story of who came before Homo sapiens.","teach":[{"title":"How Archaeologists Work","body":"Archaeologists explore early human sites to uncover clues such as tools, bones and other objects. To understand these clues better, they also experiment by making and using similar tools themselves.","visual":"⛏️"},{"title":"What Is a Fossil?","body":"A fossil is the preserved remains, traces or impressions of plants, animals or humans from the distant past. Fossils form when remains get buried under layers of earth and slowly turn into stone over thousands or millions of years.","visual":"🦴"},{"title":"Homo Habilis, the Handy Man","body":"Homo habilis ('handy man') lived in Africa, especially the Olduvai Gorge in Tanzania and Kenya, between about 2 and 6 million years ago, and made simple chopper stone tools.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"Homo Erectus","body":"Homo erectus lived in the Eastern African Rift Valley around 2 million years ago and made handaxes and cleavers. Their successors were among the first hominins to migrate out of Africa into Europe and Asia.","visual":"🚶"},{"title":"Neanderthals and Sapiens","body":"Homo neanderthalensis lived in Europe and Southwest Asia until about 40,000 years ago and made Middle Palaeolithic flake tools. Homo sapiens -- living humans -- are the only human species alive today, and developed the most complex technologies of all.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which early human species is known as the 'handy man' and made chopper stone tools at Olduvai Gorge?","options":["Homo habilis","Homo erectus","Homo neanderthalensis","Homo sapiens"],"answer":0,"explain":"Homo habilis, meaning 'handy man', made chopper stone tools in Africa's Olduvai Gorge."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Homo neanderthalensis lived in Europe and Southwest Asia until about how long ago?","options":["40,000 years ago","4,000 years ago","400,000 years ago","4 million years ago"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states Homo neanderthalensis lived until about 40,000 years ago."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the older end (in millions of years) of the date range given for Homo habilis at Olduvai Gorge?","answer":6,"explain":"Homo habilis is dated to living in Olduvai Gorge between 2 and 6 million years ago."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What term describes preserved remains or traces of plants, animals or humans from the distant past?","options":["Fossil","Artefact","Hominin","Relic"],"answer":0,"explain":"A fossil is a preserved trace of ancient life, formed over thousands or millions of years."}]},{"id":"eh3","title":"The Ages of Humankind","icon":"⏳","kind":"lesson","story":"A great stone wheel of ages spins before you -- Palaeolithic, Mesolithic, Neolithic, Chalcolithic, Bronze, Iron. Learn to read its markings, or you'll never know which age's tools you're holding.","teach":[{"title":"Palaeolithic (Old Stone Age)","body":"Palaeo means 'old' and lithic means 'stone', so Palaeolithic is the Old Stone Age. It was marked by a hunting-gathering lifestyle and the use of simple stone tools.","visual":"🪓"},{"title":"Mesolithic","body":"The Mesolithic was a transitional phase between hunting-gathering and agriculture, marked by the use of microlithic (tiny stone) tools.","visual":"🔸"},{"title":"Neolithic (New Stone Age)","body":"The Neolithic saw the shift from hunting-gathering to agriculture and settled life, the domestication of animals, and the use of polished stone tools -- a change so big it is called the Neolithic Revolution.","visual":"🌾"},{"title":"Chalcolithic (Copper and Stone Age)","body":"The Chalcolithic period saw the use of copper alongside stone tools, marking the start of early metallurgy -- the extraction and shaping of metal objects.","visual":"🥉"},{"title":"Bronze Age and Iron Age","body":"The Bronze Age brought bronze metallurgy (mixing copper and tin) along with expanding trade, towns and early civilisations. The later Iron Age brought widespread iron metallurgy, producing stronger tools, weapons and more advanced societies.","visual":"⚔️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does 'Palaeolithic' mean, based on the Greek roots palaeo (old) and lithic (stone)?","options":["Old Stone Age","New Stone Age","Copper Age","Middle Stone Age"],"answer":0,"explain":"Palaeolithic literally means the Old Stone Age."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which period is a transitional phase between hunting-gathering and agriculture, marked by microlithic tools?","options":["Mesolithic","Palaeolithic","Neolithic","Chalcolithic"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Mesolithic sits between hunting-gathering and farming, using tiny microlithic tools."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Bronze is made by mixing copper with which other metal?","options":["Tin","Iron","Zinc","Lead"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bronze metallurgy involves mixing copper and tin."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which age is characterised by the domestication of animals and a shift to agriculture and settled life?","options":["Neolithic","Palaeolithic","Mesolithic","Iron Age"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Neolithic period marked the shift to farming, settled life and animal domestication."}]},{"id":"eh4","title":"Palaeolithic Hunter-Gatherers","icon":"🪓","kind":"lesson","story":"Handaxe in hand, you join a band of hunter-gatherers moving across ancient India. From Attirampakkam to Isampur, every campsite has left behind stone tools that tell of a million-year-old struggle to survive.","teach":[{"title":"India's Oldest Sites","body":"The oldest human settlement in the Indian subcontinent dates back to about 2 million years ago. Attirampakkam in Tamil Nadu is dated to about 1.5-1.7 million years ago, and Isampur in Karnataka to about 1.2 million years ago.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"Early Tools and Their Uses","body":"At these sites, large cutting tools such as handaxes and cleavers, plus scrapers and choppers made of quartzite and limestone, have been found. These tools were used to chop animal meat, dig out tubers, scrape animal skin, and cleave bones to extract marrow.","visual":"🍖"},{"title":"Smaller, Sharper Tools","body":"Further progress in hunting and gathering led to smaller stone tools -- scrapers, borers and points -- showing improved efficiency in hunting through projectiles tipped with sharp points.","visual":"🏹"},{"title":"The Bow, Blades and Beads","body":"Later, humans invented the bow and arrow, along with parallel-sided blade and microblade tools made from glassy rocks. They also developed symbolic communication, painted cave and rock-shelter walls, used body pigments, and made the first beads of stone, bone and shell.","visual":"🎨"},{"title":"The Burin and Homo Sapiens","body":"A distinctive tool called the burin, or engraver, was used to engrave symbolic features on bones and shells such as ostrich eggs, with microblades fixed into handles. These developments are linked to Homo sapiens, who spread across the world, including Australia and the Americas, between 50,000 and 12,000 years ago.","visual":"🐚"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Palaeolithic site in Tamil Nadu is dated to about 1.5-1.7 million years ago?","options":["Attirampakkam","Isampur","Bhimbetka","Mehrgarh"],"answer":0,"explain":"Attirampakkam in Tamil Nadu is dated to about 1.5-1.7 million years ago."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What new weapon, alongside blade and microblade tools, did Upper Palaeolithic humans invent?","options":["Bow and arrow","Iron sword","Wheel","Plough"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter says humans invented the bow and arrow along with blade and microblade tools."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Isampur in Karnataka is dated to about how many million years ago?","answer":1.2,"explain":"Isampur in Karnataka is dated to about 1.2 million years ago."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What was the burin, a distinctive Palaeolithic tool, used for?","options":["Engraving symbolic features on bones and shells","Grinding grain into flour","Digging irrigation canals","Weaving cloth"],"answer":0,"explain":"The burin or engraver was used to engrave symbolic features on bones and shells like ostrich eggs."}]},{"id":"eh5","title":"Mesolithic Hunter-Gatherers","icon":"🏹","kind":"lesson","story":"The ice is melting, and forests are creeping back across the land. Follow the rivers to Bhimbetka, where painted rock shelters and tiny microlithic tools mark humanity's first great population boom.","teach":[{"title":"A Warming World","body":"Around 12000 years ago, Earth's climate became warmer, and forests and grasslands expanded into areas once covered by ice sheets. The new landscapes offered a wider variety of resources, sparking the first-ever population explosion in human history.","visual":"🌡️"},{"title":"Microliths and Fishing","body":"A variety of microlithic tools let people gather aquatic food, both marine and freshwater, and fishing became the mainstay of their subsistence economy.","visual":"🐟"},{"title":"Art and New Habitats","body":"Art activity flourished during this time, and new habitats such as caves and rock shelters were frequently occupied.","visual":"🎨"},{"title":"Bhimbetka","body":"The World Heritage Site of Bhimbetka in Madhya Pradesh contains hundreds of painted rock shelters with Mesolithic and even earlier human occupation.","visual":"🷓"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"About how many years ago did Earth's climate warm, expanding forests and grasslands and marking the Mesolithic changes described in the chapter?","options":["12000 years ago","2 million years ago","5000 years ago","125000 years ago"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter dates this warming to around 12000 years ago."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which World Heritage Site in Madhya Pradesh contains hundreds of painted rock shelters from the Mesolithic period?","options":["Bhimbetka","Mehrgarh","Dholavira","Sanganakallu"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bhimbetka in Madhya Pradesh is famous for its painted Mesolithic rock shelters."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What kind of tools are characteristic of the Mesolithic period?","options":["Microlithic (tiny stone) tools","Handaxes and cleavers","Polished stone tools","Bronze tools"],"answer":0,"explain":"Microlithic, or tiny stone, tools mark the Mesolithic period."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What became the mainstay of the Mesolithic subsistence economy thanks to new aquatic resources?","options":["Fishing","Farming","Cattle herding","Long-distance trade"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states fishing was the mainstay of the Mesolithic subsistence economy."}]},{"id":"eh6","title":"The Neolithic Revolution","icon":"🌾","kind":"lesson","story":"At Mehrgarh on the Bolan River, mud-brick houses and granaries rise where hunter-gatherers once roamed. You are about to witness a revolution -- not of armies, but of wheat, barley, sheep and settled village life.","teach":[{"title":"What Was the Neolithic Revolution?","body":"As hunter-gatherers grew familiar with seasons and food resources, they gradually shifted to a food-producing way of life. The hallmark of this Neolithic Revolution was the domestication of select animals and plants, and the development of new breeds through cultivation and husbandry.","visual":"🐐"},{"title":"Farmers, Pottery and Villages","body":"While hunter-gatherers made tools for procuring food, Neolithic farmers made tools for producing and processing food, and developed earthenware pottery in various shapes and sizes. They established the first village settlements, laying the foundations for the later urban revolution.","visual":"🿺"},{"title":"Mehrgarh","body":"Mehrgarh, on the Bolan River in present-day Pakistan, is the oldest Neolithic site and earliest agricultural village of the Indian subcontinent, dating back to about 7000 BCE. Its people built sun-dried mud-brick houses and granaries, buried their dead in graves, and made ornaments from semi-precious stones like lapis lazuli and carnelian.","visual":"🧱"},{"title":"Farming and the First Metal","body":"The people of Mehrgarh cultivated wheat and barley, and raised sheep, goats and Indian cattle, especially the zebu humped bull. They were also the first to make copper objects, becoming Chalcolithic by about 4000 BCE and laying the basis for the Bronze Age Sindhu-Sarasvati civilisation around 3500 BCE.","visual":"🌾"},{"title":"Neolithic India by 2500 BCE","body":"By 2500 BCE, most of the Indian subcontinent was occupied by Neolithic agricultural communities, marked by cattle, sheep and goat herding along with the cultivation of cereals, millets and pulses.","visual":"🐄"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the hallmark of the Neolithic Revolution?","options":["Domestication of animals and plants","Invention of writing","Discovery of fire","Use of iron tools"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Neolithic Revolution's hallmark was bringing select animals and plants under human control."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which site on the Bolan River is the oldest Neolithic site and earliest agricultural village of the Indian subcontinent?","options":["Mehrgarh","Harappa","Bhimbetka","Sanganakallu"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mehrgarh on the Bolan River is the oldest Neolithic site and earliest agricultural village."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Around what year BCE does Mehrgarh's earliest agricultural village date back to?","answer":7000,"explain":"Mehrgarh's earliest agricultural village dates back to about 7000 BCE."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which two crops did the people of Mehrgarh cultivate?","options":["Wheat and barley","Rice and millet","Maize and beans","Cotton and sugarcane"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mehrgarh's people cultivated wheat and barley."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By about 4000 BCE, the people of Mehrgarh became the first to make objects from which metal, entering the metal age?","options":["Copper","Iron","Bronze","Gold"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mehrgarh's people were the first to make copper objects, becoming Chalcolithic by about 4000 BCE."}]},{"id":"eh7","title":"Sindhu-Sarasvati Civilisation","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"From the copper workshops of the Early Harappans to the great tanks of Dholavira, the Sindhu-Sarasvati civilisation is rising. Weigh your trade goods carefully -- the Harappans will not accept a merchant who cannot count in their standard weights.","teach":[{"title":"Early Harappan Beginnings","body":"Some Neolithic settlements in the Indus and Ghaggar-Sarasvati basins mastered copper extraction around 4000 BCE, becoming the earliest Chalcolithic sites and marking the start of the Bronze Age there. Radiocarbon dates from sites like Bhirrana and Kunal suggest a Pre-Harappan phase began between 7000 and 5500 BCE.","visual":"⛏️"},{"title":"From Early to Mature Harappan","body":"Regional pottery styles and continuity in beads, shell bangles, terracotta and copper working evolved into standard Harappan features by about 2500 BCE. Features such as perimeter walls, seals and even the Harappan script probably began in this Early Harappan stage.","visual":"🏺"},{"title":"The Harappan Timeline","body":"The civilisation's story runs from the Neolithic (7000 BCE) through the Chalcolithic (4500 BCE) and Early Harappan stage, into the Mature Harappan phase from 2600 BCE, the Late Harappan from 1900 BCE, ending by about 1300 BCE.","visual":"⏳"},{"title":"Standard Weights","body":"Harappans used their own standard systems of weight and measurement, following a binary multiple (1, 2, 4, 8, 16...) system for smaller units and multiples of ten for larger denominations, using cubical stone weights -- essential for their interregional trade.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Water Management and Farming","body":"Dholavira in Kachchh had an elaborate water harvesting system of interconnected stone and mud-brick tanks, while Lothal had a huge dockyard built entirely of burnt bricks. At Kalibangan, ploughed fields with horizontal and vertical furrows -- nearly 5000 years old -- show double-crop cultivation like today's Rabi and Kharif seasons.","visual":"💧"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"By about which year BCE had the Mature Harappan phase of the Sindhu-Sarasvati civilisation begun, per the chapter's timeline?","options":["2600 BCE","7000 BCE","1300 BCE","3500 BCE"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter's timeline places the Mature Harappan phase from 2600 BCE."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What numbering system did the Harappans use for weighing smaller units?","options":["Binary multiples (1, 2, 4, 8, 16...)","Decimal system only","Roman numerals","Base-60 system"],"answer":0,"explain":"Harappans followed a binary multiple system for smaller weight units."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Harappan site is famous for its elaborate water harvesting system of interconnected stone and mud-brick tanks?","options":["Dholavira","Lothal","Kalibangan","Mehrgarh"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dholavira in Kachchh had an elaborate water harvesting system of tanks fed by dams and canals."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Ploughed fields nearly 5000 years old, showing evidence of double-crop cultivation, were found at which site?","options":["Kalibangan","Lothal","Harappa","Dholavira"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Early Harappans ploughed fields at Kalibangan showing horizontal and vertical furrows."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Harappan site had a huge dockyard built entirely of burnt bricks?","options":["Lothal","Dholavira","Mohenjo-Daro","Rakhigarhi"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lothal's dockyard, built entirely of burnt bricks, showcases Harappan architectural skill."}]},{"id":"eh8","title":"Mesopotamia, Egypt and China","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"Three more river-valley worlds await: the ziggurats of Sumer, the pyramids of the Nile, and the oracle bones of the Yellow River. Cross each civilisation and learn how rivers built empires far from the Sindhu-Sarasvati.","teach":[{"title":"Four River Civilisations","body":"Four early world civilisations emerged in river plains: the Harappan along the Sindhu and Sarasvati, the Mesopotamian around the Euphrates and Tigris, the Egyptian along the Nile, and the Chinese in the Huang He basin. Mesopotamia's closeness to the Indus-Sarasvati region led to strong trade contacts between the two.","visual":"🌊"},{"title":"Sumerians: Cities and Cuneiform","body":"The Sumerians built the earliest city-based civilisation at Ur and other cities in Sumer, and were the first to build dams and canals for irrigation and to use mud and burnt bricks. Each city built a grand temple, a ziggurat, for its chief god; around 3200 BCE the Sumerians began writing in cuneiform, wedge-shaped marks pressed into clay tablets, which spread across Mesopotamia by 3000 BCE.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Akkadians, Assyrians, Babylonians","body":"The Sumerians were overshadowed in 2334 BCE by the Akkadians, whose king Sargon's records mention trade with Meluhha (the Sindhu-Sarasvati region), Dilmun (Bahrain) and Magan (Oman). The Assyrians rose in 2154 BCE, and the Babylonians from 1900 BCE, whose king Hammurabi (from 1792 BCE) compiled the Code of Hammurabi, a set of rules for civil and social conduct.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"Egypt: The Nile and the Pharaohs","body":"Egyptian civilisation grew along the Nile, whose annual floods deposited fertile black mud called 'kemet'. Powerful rulers called Pharaohs were buried under mastaba tombs that evolved into pyramids, such as the step pyramid at Saqqara, reflecting the belief that a person's ka lived on after death if the body was preserved through mummification.","visual":"🔺"},{"title":"Decoding Egypt's Script","body":"Egyptian history is reconstructed partly from papyrus records. In 1799, Pierre Bouchard found the Rosetta Stone, carrying the same text in three scripts including Greek; in 1822 the French linguist Jean-Francois Champollion used it to finally decipher Egyptian hieroglyphic writing.","visual":"🪨"},{"title":"China: Rivers, Dynasties and Oracles","body":"Chinese civilisation flourished along the Huang He (Yellow River) and the Yangtze, with Neolithic cultures from around 7000 BCE and urban centres from about 1600 BCE. The Shang (1600-1046 BCE) and Zhou (1046-256 BCE) dynasties were major Bronze Age dynasties, and the earliest Chinese records come from 'oracle bones' -- animal bones and tortoise shells heated until they cracked, with the cracks interpreted to foretell the future.","visual":"🐉"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Along which two rivers did the Mesopotamian civilisation develop?","options":["The Euphrates and Tigris","The Nile","The Indus and Sarasvati","The Huang He and Yangtze"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mesopotamia was the land drained by the Euphrates and Tigris rivers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who first developed cuneiform, one of the earliest writing systems, around 3200 BCE?","options":["The Sumerians","The Egyptians","The Akkadians","The Chinese"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Sumerians were the first to start writing in cuneiform around 3200 BCE."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What was the Code of Hammurabi?","options":["A compilation of rules and regulations for civil and social conduct","A religious hymn book","A trade agreement with Egypt","A system of writing"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hammurabi compiled rules and regulations for civil and social conduct throughout his empire."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who deciphered Egyptian hieroglyphic writing using the Rosetta Stone, in 1822?","options":["Jean-Francois Champollion","Pierre Bouchard","Herodotus","Sargon"],"answer":0,"explain":"The French linguist Jean-Francois Champollion deciphered the Egyptian script in 1822."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which two Bronze Age Chinese dynasties does the chapter name, in chronological order?","options":["Shang and Zhou","Qin and Han","Ming and Qing","Tang and Song"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Shang (1600-1046 BCE) and Zhou (1046-256 BCE) dynasties were the major Bronze Age dynasties."}]},{"id":"eh-boss","title":"The Chronicler's Challenge","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"The Chronicler of Ages guards the gateway out of prehistory, holding every hominin, tool and civilisation you have studied in a single test of memory. Answer well, and you carry humanity's story into the age of writing.","boss":{"name":"The Chronicler of Ages","taunt":"Six ages, four river civilisations -- name them all correctly, or be forgotten like an undeciphered script!"},"teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which hominin was the first to migrate out of Africa, using handaxes and cleavers?","options":["Homo erectus","Homo habilis","Homo sapiens","Homo neanderthalensis"],"answer":0,"explain":"Homo erectus was the first hominin to exit Africa with hand axes and cleavers."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which age is marked by mixing copper and tin to make bronze, alongside expanding trade and towns?","options":["Bronze Age","Chalcolithic","Iron Age","Neolithic"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Bronze Age involved bronze metallurgy and the growth of trade, towns and civilisation."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which site is the oldest Neolithic settlement and earliest agricultural village of the Indian subcontinent?","options":["Mehrgarh","Harappa","Bhimbetka","Kalibangan"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mehrgarh on the Bolan River is the oldest Neolithic site and earliest agricultural village."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Around what year BCE did the Mature Harappan phase of the Sindhu-Sarasvati civilisation begin, per the chapter's timeline?","answer":2600,"explain":"The chapter's timeline places the Mature Harappan phase from 2600 BCE."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Mesopotamian ruler compiled the famous Code of Hammurabi?","options":["Hammurabi","Sargon","Champollion","Cleopatra"],"answer":0,"explain":"Hammurabi, king of Babylonia from 1792 BCE, compiled the Code of Hammurabi."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Along which river did the Egyptian civilisation flourish, whose annual floods deposited fertile black mud called 'kemet'?","options":["The Nile","The Euphrates","The Tigris","The Yangtze"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Nile's annual floods deposited fertile black mud, kemet, across Egypt."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which script, deciphered using the Rosetta Stone, was used in ancient Egypt?","options":["Hieroglyphic script","Cuneiform script","Harappan script (Sindhu lipi)","Oracle bone script"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Rosetta Stone helped decipher ancient Egypt's hieroglyphic script."}]}]},{"id":"stateandsociety9","index":4,"name":"State and Society up to 1000 CE","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 5","emoji":"🏛️","palette":{"from":"#facc15","to":"#713f12"},"lessons":[{"id":"ss1","title":"Clans, Chants and Councils","icon":"📜","kind":"lesson","story":"You step out of the timestream into the Sapta-Sindhu region, where hymns are being chanted around a sacred fire and clan elders argue in a crowded assembly. To understand how India's story of states begins, you must first learn how these clans governed themselves.","teach":[{"title":"The Four Vedas","body":"The Vedas are the earliest known corpus of Indian literature, existing in oral form for centuries before being written down. The Rigveda, the earliest Veda, contains 1,028 hymns (sūktas) composed in the Sapta-Sindhu region. The Samaveda, Yajurveda, and Atharvaveda followed, each serving different ritual and practical purposes.","visual":"📖"},{"title":"Anatomy of a Veda","body":"Each Vedic text is made up of four parts: the samhitā (hymns for rituals), the brāhmaṇa (prose explanations of rituals), the āraṇyaka (forest philosophy), and the upaniṣhad (reflections on the Self and the Ultimate Being, Brāhmaṇ).","visual":"🕉️"},{"title":"Janas and the Pañchajana","body":"Early Vedic society was organised into janas, or clans, bound by kinship ties. The Rigveda mentions about thirty janas, five of which — the Yadu, Turvaśha, Puru, Anu, and Druhyu — were together known as the pañchajana ('five peoples'). The name 'Bharata' first appears in the Rigveda in reference to the Bharata jana.","visual":"🏕️"},{"title":"Three Assemblies","body":"The rājā acted mainly as a clan chief, leading in war and protecting his people. Alongside him stood three assemblies: the sabhā (a smaller body of select elites with a judicial role), the samiti (a larger assembly of the broader population focused on policy), and the vidhata (a popular gathering that discussed warfare and political matters).","visual":"⚖️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How many hymns (sūktas) does the Rigveda contain?","options":["1,028 sūktas","500 sūktas","2,000 sūktas","108 sūktas"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Rigveda consists of 1,028 hymns, some praising deities and others reflecting on universal themes like creation and death."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which five janas together were known as the pañchajana?","options":["Yadu, Turvaśha, Puru, Anu, and Druhyu","Kuru, Pañchāla, Magadha, Kośhala, Avanti","Chola, Chera, Pandya, Satiyaputra, Kāmboja","Anga, Vanga, Kalinga, Magadha, Vatsa"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Rigveda names about thirty janas, five of which — Yadu, Turvaśha, Puru, Anu, and Druhyu — were collectively called the pañchajana."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Vedic assembly was a smaller body of select elites that primarily served a judicial function?","options":["Sabhā","Samiti","Vidhata","Mantri-pariṣhad"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sabhā was a smaller, elite body with a judicial role, distinct from the larger, policy-focused samiti and the popular vidhata."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What did the term jana originally refer to in early Vedic society?","options":["A clan or kin-based group of people","A fortified city","A tax-collecting district","A merchant guild"],"answer":0,"explain":"Janas were groups of people bound together by kinship ties, the basic political-social unit of early Vedic society."}]},{"id":"ss2","title":"From Janapada to Empire","icon":"🗺️","kind":"lesson","story":"Centuries roll forward. Iron tools glint in Ganga plain fields, and PGW pottery gives way to glossy black NBPW ware. Clans have grown into kingdoms with names like Magadha and Kosala — and far south, three crowned kings rule the fertile land of Tamilakam.","teach":[{"title":"Janapada to Mahājanapada","body":"The word janapada means 'where a people (jana) first set its feet', marking a shift from kinship identity to territorial identity, roughly between 1000 and 600 BCE. Between about 600 BCE and 300 CE, this grew into bigger political units called mahājanapadas. Historical sources usually speak of sixteen mahājanapadas, of which Magadha (in present-day Bihar) gradually became the most powerful.","visual":"🌾"},{"title":"Monarchies and Republics","body":"The political landscape of northern India included two forms of organisation: monarchical states (rājyas) ruled by kings, and republican states (gaṇas or saṁghas) governed collectively.","visual":"👑"},{"title":"The Satavāhanas of the Deccan","body":"In southern India, the Mauryan Empire was succeeded in the Deccan by the Satavāhana Empire, which lasted nearly 500 years, from the 2nd century BCE to the 3rd century CE.","visual":"🻺"},{"title":"Kingdoms of Tamilakam","body":"Further south, the Cholas (lower Kaveri valley, tiger emblem), Pandyas (Tamraparni and Vaigai valleys, fish emblem), Keralaputras/Cheras (Kerala, bow emblem), and Satiyaputras (northern Tamil Nadu) flourished between 300 BCE and 300 CE. The Chera, Chola, and Pandya rulers were called the three crowned kings, or 'Vendar of Tamilakam', and are known largely through Sangam literature.","visual":"🐅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Historical sources usually speak of how many mahājanapadas?","options":["Sixteen","Ten","Twenty-five","Six"],"answer":0,"explain":"Contemporary texts mention sixteen mahājanapadas that emerged between roughly 600 BCE and 300 CE."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which mahājanapada, located in present-day Bihar, gradually emerged as the most powerful?","options":["Magadha","Kośhala","Avanti","Vatsa"],"answer":0,"explain":"Magadha's strategic location, fertile plains, and strong rulers enabled it to expand and eventually give rise to the Mauryan Empire."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which empire succeeded the Mauryas in the Deccan and lasted nearly 500 years?","options":["The Satavāhana Empire","The Kuṣhāṇa Empire","The Gupta Empire","The Chola Empire"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Satavāhana Empire ruled the Deccan from about the 2nd century BCE to the 3rd century CE."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The tiger was the royal emblem of which southern kingdom?","options":["The Cholas","The Cheras","The Pandyas","The Satavāhanas"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Cholas used the tiger emblem, while the Cheras used the bow and the Pandyas the fish."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What term describes the republican states of northern India during this period?","options":["Gaṇas or saṁghas","Bhuktis","Āhāras","Agrahārams"],"answer":0,"explain":"Alongside monarchical rājyas, northern India also had republican states known as gaṇas or saṁghas."}]},{"id":"ss3","title":"What Makes a King?","icon":"👑","kind":"lesson","story":"You are granted an audience with a mahārāja preparing for his coronation. Priests recite oaths about justice, advisors quote the Arthaśhāstra, and everyone speaks of an ideal ruler whose authority might one day stretch across the entire subcontinent.","teach":[{"title":"Duties of the King","body":"The king was responsible for protecting subjects from external threats and internal disorder, and for administering justice in cases such as abduction, robbery, theft, and adultery. Serious offences — including killing a cow, treachery, and drinking intoxicating liquor — carried severe punishments, sometimes even capital punishment.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Hereditary, but Not Absolute","body":"While kingship was generally hereditary, early texts also refer to kings being elected or even expelled, showing that royal authority was not always absolute in practice.","visual":"🪑"},{"title":"Guiding Texts","body":"Kauṭilya's Arthaśhāstra states, 'Only if a king is himself energetically active, do his officers follow him energetically.' The Yajurveda's coronation oath advises the king to judge the strong and weak impartially and protect the country from calamities. The Śhānti Parva of the Mahābhārata guides rulers on ethical conduct and justice.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"The Chakravarti Ideal","body":"Early Indian kings imagined authority over the whole subcontinent, expressed through terms like Jambudvīpa, Bhāratavarṣha, the Aśhvamedha and Rājasūya yajñas, Prithivi, and chakravarti kśhetra — the Arthaśhāstra defines this as the land 'between the Himavat and the sea', the domain of a universal paramount ruler. The Chera king Nedunjeral Adan won the title adhirāja and reportedly reached the Himalayas, while the Chola Rajendra I took the title Gangaikonda in the 11th century CE to mark his conquest along the Ganga.","visual":"🌏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Arthaśhāstra, what is the chakravarti kśhetra?","options":["The domain of a universal paramount ruler, between the Himavat and the sea","A district governed by a pradeśhika","The territory managed by a village assembly","A fortified royal capital"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Arthaśhāstra equates Prithivi with chakravarti kśhetra, defining it as the land between the Himalayas and the sea."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which title did the Chola ruler Rajendra I adopt in the 11th century CE to commemorate his conquest of regions along the Ganga?","options":["Gangaikonda","Adhirāja","Chakravarti","Samrāṭ"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rajendra I adopted the title Gangaikonda ('bringer of the Ganga') after his campaign reached the Ganga."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which text advises the king to 'judge the strong and weak impartially and fairly'?","options":["The Yajurveda's coronation oath","The Arthaśhāstra alone","The Purāṇas","Sangam poems"],"answer":0,"explain":"The oath of coronation in the Yajurveda instructs the king to judge impartially and protect the country from calamities."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Was kingship in early India entirely hereditary in practice?","options":["No — there are references to kings being elected or even expelled","Yes — succession always followed strict birth order","No — kings were always chosen by a guild vote","Yes — only a queen's firstborn could inherit"],"answer":0,"explain":"Although generally hereditary, sources mention kings being elected or expelled, showing royal authority was not always absolute."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which section of the Mahābhārata offers guidance to rulers on ethical conduct, justice, and their responsibilities to subjects?","options":["Śhānti Parva","Bhagavad Gītā","Udyoga Parva","Ādi Parva"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Śhānti Parva of the Mahābhārata is devoted to guiding rulers on ethics, justice, and governance."}]},{"id":"ss4","title":"Seven Pillars of the State","icon":"🏰","kind":"lesson","story":"Kauṭilya himself sits you down and sketches a diagram: a king surrounded by six other elements, like spokes of a wheel. 'Remove one,' he warns, 'and the carriage does not move.' Nearby, a strange rock near Girnar carries the messages of three different rulers, centuries apart.","teach":[{"title":"Kauṭilya's Saptāṁga","body":"Kauṭilya viewed the state as an organic whole made of seven constituents (Saptāṁga): the king (swāmi), the councillors and ministers (amātya), the territory and its people (janapada), the forts (durga), the treasury (kośha), the forces of defence and law and order (daṇḍa), and the allies (mitra).","visual":"🧩"},{"title":"The Council of Ministers","body":"The king governed through a multi-layered system centred on the mantri-pariṣhad, a small body of elder statesmen who advised him. It generally included the treasurer, the chief tax collector, the chief legal advisor, and the commander-in-chief of the army.","visual":"🗣️"},{"title":"'One Wheel Does Not Move the Carriage'","body":"This Arthaśhāstra line captures the belief that effective governance needs cooperation and a well-organised administrative machinery. An Ashokan inscription confirms that the council of ministers could take decisions independently in the public interest during the emperor's absence.","visual":"🛺"},{"title":"The Junagadh Rock Inscription","body":"Near Girnar in Gujarat, a single rock bears inscriptions of three rulers across roughly 700 years: the Mauryan emperor Aśhoka (3rd century BCE, Major Rock Edicts on Dhamma), the Western Satrap Rudradaman I (c. 150 CE, who repaired the Sudarshana Lake), and the Gupta emperor Skandagupta (5th century CE, who restored the lake again after damage).","visual":"🪨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Kauṭilya's Saptāṁga, what does kośha refer to?","options":["The treasury or wealth of the kingdom","The army","The allies","The territory"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kośha is the treasury or wealth of the kingdom, one of the seven constituents of the state."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many constituents make up Kauṭilya's Saptāṁga, the 'seven limbs' of the state?","answer":7,"explain":"Saptāṁga literally means 'seven limbs': king, ministers, territory, forts, treasury, army, and allies."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which three rulers left inscriptions on the Junagadh Rock near Girnar?","options":["Aśhoka, Rudradaman I, and Skandagupta","Chandragupta Maurya, Aśhoka, and Samudragupta","Bindusāra, Kaniṣhka, and Harṣha","Pulakeśhin I, Rudradaman I, and Rājarāja I"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Junagadh Rock preserves records of Aśhoka, the Western Satrap Rudradaman I, and the Gupta emperor Skandagupta."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What did the mantri-pariṣhad (council of ministers) typically consist of?","options":["A small body of elder statesmen including the treasurer, chief tax collector, chief legal advisor, and commander-in-chief","An elected assembly of all village householders","A rotating body of foreign ambassadors","A hereditary council of priests only"],"answer":0,"explain":"The council of ministers was a small body of elder statesmen who advised and supported the king."}]},{"id":"ss5","title":"Running an Empire","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"An empire is only as strong as the officers who run its villages. You follow a district governor on his rounds — through Satavāhana āhāras, Gupta districts with copper-plate records, and finally into a Tamil village where a child draws names from a pot to choose its rulers.","teach":[{"title":"Satavāhana Administration","body":"The Satavāhana empire was divided into āhāras (administrative divisions), each with its own ministers (amātyas). Below them were villages led by a headman called the grāmika. District governors, or pradeśhikas, handled judicial and administrative work, often consulting important residents such as bankers, caravan leaders, artisans, and scribes.","visual":"🏘️"},{"title":"Decentralisation, c. 300-800 CE","body":"Kingdoms (rājya) were divided into provinces called bhuktis in the north and maṇḍalas or maṇḍalams in the south; these were subdivided into viṣhaya or bhoga (north) and koṭṭams or valanāḍu (south); below that were districts — adhiṣhṭhāna or paṭṭana (north) and nāḍu (south); and groups of villages, called vīthis (north) or paṭṭalā and kūṛram (south). The village remained the smallest unit.","visual":"📐"},{"title":"Gupta Administration","body":"The Guptas retained much of the earlier system: the mantri headed civil administration, alongside the commander-in-chief, the general, and the chief of palace guards. A new post, sāndhivigrahika ('minister of peace and war'), was introduced. The Damodarpur copper plates (Kumāragupta I's reign) record a five-member district office: the head district officer, chief banker, chief caravan trader, chief artisan, and chief of revenue collection.","visual":"🖋️"},{"title":"South Indian Variations","body":"The Pallavas (c. 275-897 CE) gave tax-free Brahmadeya village grants, managed by village committees called variyams. The Chālukyas of Badami (c. 543-753 CE) instead granted land to Brahmin settlements called agrahārams, some of which, like Aihole and Badami, became centres of learning. Later, the Gurjara-Pratihāras, Pālas, and Rāṣhṭrakūṭas fought the 'tri-partite struggle' to control the capital Kannauj.","visual":"⚔️"},{"title":"Chola Village Democracy","body":"The Chola empire was divided into maṇḍalams (provinces), valanāḍus (districts), nāḍus (groups of villages), and finally urs (individual villages). The 10th-century Uttaramerur inscription of Parāntaka I describes the Kudavolai ('ballot pot') system: candidate names on palm leaves were placed in a pot and drawn by a child in public to select members for village committees (variyams) handling irrigation, justice, and tax collection.","visual":"🗳️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Satavāhana administration, what was an āhāra?","options":["An administrative division, headed by amātyas","A village council","A trade guild","A unit of currency"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Satavāhana empire was divided into āhāras, each with its own ministers (amātyas)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What were provinces called in the north Indian administrative system of roughly 300-800 CE?","options":["Bhuktis","Maṇḍalams","Valanāḍus","Āhāras"],"answer":0,"explain":"In the north, provinces were called bhuktis; in the south, they were maṇḍalas or maṇḍalams."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What new administrative post, meaning 'minister of peace and war', was introduced during the Gupta period?","options":["Sāndhivigrahika","Pradeśhika","Grāmika","Viśhapati"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sāndhivigrahika was a new Gupta-era post responsible for peace and war matters."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What were the tax-free village land grants of the Pallava dynasty called?","options":["Brahmadeya villages","Agrahārams","Kudavolai villages","Variyam villages"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Pallavas granted tax-free Brahmadeya villages, while the Chālukyas later granted agrahārams."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What was the purpose of the Kudavolai system described in the Uttaramerur inscription?","options":["Selecting village assembly representatives by drawing palm-leaf ballots from a pot","Collecting land revenue from farmers","Recording royal military victories","Regulating guild membership fees"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Kudavolai, or 'ballot pot' system, was used to fairly select members of Chola village assemblies and their committees."}]},{"id":"ss6","title":"Dharma and the Social Order","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"A sage explains to you that dharma is not a religion but something that 'upholds beings' — and that the four varṇas were meant to describe roles, not birth-fixed cages. You watch as weavers-turned-archers and brāhmaṇas-turned-land-managers prove that the boundaries were never as rigid as they seemed.","teach":[{"title":"Samatva and Ṛita","body":"Samatva, the principle of sameness, holds that all bodies are made of the same matter and all consciousness is a manifestation of one supreme consciousness. Ṛita, described in the Ṛigveda, is the all-pervading cosmic order that keeps nature and society in balance.","visual":"☯️"},{"title":"What is Dharma?","body":"Dharma does not mean religion; it refers to duty, obligation, righteousness, and moral conduct, derived from the Sanskrit root dhri (also the root of dharati, 'the earth'). In the Mahābhārata, Bhīṣhma says dharma 'is that which upholds beings; whatever upholds all beings is dharma.'","visual":"🌿"},{"title":"The Varṇa System","body":"The four varṇas were brāhmaṇas (study and teach the Vedas, perform yajñas), kṣhatriyas (warfare, justice, protection), vaiśhyas (agriculture, pastoralism, trade), and śhūdras (expected to assist the others, but in practice also farmers, herders, and artisans). The Puruṣhasūkta in Book 10 of the Ṛigveda holds the earliest reference to varṇa. The Buddhist Sutta Nipāta states: 'No brāhmaṇa is such by birth... A brāhmaṇa is such by his deeds.'","visual":"🔱"},{"title":"From Varṇa to Jāti","body":"Jāti emerged over time through intermarriage among varṇas, migrating communities becoming endogamous, and territorial differences. The number of varṇas stayed fixed at four, but there was no limit on the number of jātis, which kept growing as new occupations developed.","visual":"🧵"},{"title":"Social Mobility in Practice","body":"Several ruling dynasties — the Nandas, Mauryas, Śhuṅgas, Satavāhanas, Vākāṭakas, Guptas, and Puṣhyabhūtis — came from diverse social backgrounds. The Mandsaur Stone Inscription (473 CE) records a guild of silk weavers who were also skilled in archery and astrology, while the Karitalai copper plates of Mahārāja Jayanātha (late 5th century CE) record brāhmaṇas working as land managers.","visual":"📈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does dharma primarily mean in this chapter's usage, rather than 'religion'?","options":["Duty, obligation, righteousness, and moral conduct","A fixed set of religious rituals","Military strategy","Tax law"],"answer":0,"explain":"Dharma refers to duty and righteous conduct, derived from the root dhri, meaning 'to uphold'."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Where does the earliest reference to the four varṇas appear?","options":["The Puruṣhasūkta in Book 10 of the Ṛigveda","The Arthaśhāstra","The Uttaramerur inscription","The Milindapañho"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Puruṣhasūkta hymn in Book 10 of the Ṛigveda contains the earliest known reference to the four varṇas."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Buddhist text Sutta Nipāta, what determines whether someone is a brāhmaṇa?","options":["Their deeds, not their birth","Their birth alone","Their wealth","Royal appointment"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Sutta Nipāta states that no one is a brāhmaṇa by birth, but by deeds."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What factors contributed to the emergence of distinct jātis?","options":["Intermarriage among varṇas, migrating communities becoming endogamous, and territorial differences","A royal decree fixing occupations by law","The introduction of coinage","The spread of Buddhism alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Jāti developed gradually through intermarriage, endogamy among migrating groups, and regional differences."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Mandsaur Stone Inscription (473 CE) records a guild of silk weavers who had also become skilled in which other pursuits?","options":["Archery and astrology","Shipbuilding and navigation","Medicine and surgery","Pottery and metallurgy"],"answer":0,"explain":"The inscription shows the weavers' guild, which had migrated from Lata to Dashapura, was also proficient in archery and astrology."}]},{"id":"ss7","title":"Family, Women and Faith","icon":"🪔","kind":"lesson","story":"In a village courtyard you meet a household spanning generations — grandparents entering the forest stage of life, a mother reciting Vedic hymns first composed by women sages, and children learning that the fire ritual now shares space with something new: songs of devotion to Vishnu and Shiva.","teach":[{"title":"Building Blocks of Society","body":"The kula (family) was the smallest social unit, linked to the grāma (village), which consisted of several families. A group of grāmas formed a viśha, headed by a viśhapati; above that stood the jana, with the rājā as its chief protector.","visual":"🏡"},{"title":"Āśhramas and Puruṣhārthas","body":"Life was divided into four āśhramas: brahmacharya (studentship), gṛihastha (householder), vānaprastha (forest life), and saṁnyāsa (renunciation), accompanied by the ṣhoḍaśha saṁskāras, or sixteen rites of passage. The four goals of life (puruṣhārthas) were dharma, artha (material well-being), kāma (desires), and mokṣha (liberation).","visual":"🧘"},{"title":"Women in Vedic and Later Society","body":"Vedic women took part in scholarly learning, rituals, chariot races, and the sabhā; hymns are attributed to women sages Apālā, Viśhvavārā, Ghoṣhā, and Lopāmudrā. The Manu-smṛiti (3.56) says where women are honoured, 'there gods rejoice.' Later, Prabhāvatī Gupta (daughter of Chandragupta II) ruled as regent in the Vākāṭaka kingdom; Sangam poetess Avvaiyar composed literature; and Chola queen Sembiyan Mahādevī patronised temple building.","visual":"👩"},{"title":"From Yajña to Bhakti","body":"Vedic worship centred on nature deities honoured through yajñas. Over time, devotion turned toward personal gods like Viṣhṇu, Śhiva, and Śhakti. Bhakti offered a direct connection to god without elaborate ritual, open to all regardless of class or gender, and became organised in the 6th-century Tamil region through the twelve Āḻvārs (devoted to Viṣhṇu) and sixty-three Nāyanmārs (devoted to Śhiva).","visual":"🙏"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In the Vedic kinship system, what was the smallest social unit?","options":["The kula (family)","The janapada","The viśha","The jana"],"answer":0,"explain":"The kula, or family, was the smallest and most basic social unit, connected to the grāma (village) above it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which four goals of life (puruṣhārthas) guided an individual's life?","options":["Dharma, artha, kāma, and mokṣha","Sabhā, samiti, vidhata, and rājā","Varṇa, jāti, gotra, and āśhrama","Yajña, dāna, tapa, and satya"],"answer":0,"explain":"The four puruṣhārthas were dharma (righteousness), artha (material well-being), kāma (desire), and mokṣha (liberation)."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who ruled as a regent in the Vākāṭaka kingdom and issued land grants in her own name?","options":["Prabhāvatī Gupta","Sembiyan Mahādevī","Lopāmudrā","Avvaiyar"],"answer":0,"explain":"Prabhāvatī Gupta, daughter of the Gupta ruler Chandragupta II, ruled as regent in the Vākāṭaka kingdom."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What made the Bhakti tradition distinctive compared to Vedic ritual worship?","options":["It offered a direct connection to god without elaborate rituals, open to all regardless of class or gender","It required elaborate yajñas performed only by brāhmaṇas","It was restricted to kings and nobles","It replaced all earlier deities with a single new god"],"answer":0,"explain":"Bhakti offered a path to the divine that did not require elaborate Vedic rituals and was accessible to everyone."}]},{"id":"ss8","title":"Scholars, Farmers and Traders","icon":"🎓","kind":"lesson","story":"Your journey ends at a bustling gurukula beside a busy trade route. Students recite grammar and astronomy under a teacher's watchful eye, farmers argue over the one-sixth land tax, and a guild master counts coins destined for a Buddhist monastery's endowment.","teach":[{"title":"The Gurukula","body":"Education was holistic, cultivating truth, patience, self-control, and purity of mind (sattvaśhuddhi). Students studied the Vedas, grammar, logic, mathematics, medicine, and astronomy, alongside music, dance, and archery. The guru-śhiṣhya paramparā treated the student as part of the teacher's own family.","visual":"📚"},{"title":"Great Centres of Learning","body":"Famous institutions included Takṣhaśhilā, Nālandā (established 427 CE), Vallabhī (established 480 CE), and Vikramśhilā (783-820 CE), which attracted students from across the subcontinent and beyond.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Farming the Land","body":"The Mauryan state taxed land at roughly one-sixth of the produce, with Kauṭilya classifying land into cultivated, wasteland/fallow, high-and-dry, sown, and grove categories. Irrigation mattered too: the governor Puṣhyagupta built a dam on Sudarshana Lake under Chandragupta Maurya, and the Cholas built the Grand Anicut (Kallanai), still in use today.","visual":"🌾"},{"title":"Trade, Ports and Guilds","body":"The two great land routes, Dakṣhiṇāpatha and Uttarāpatha, linked the subcontinent, while ports like Muziris, Kāveripaṭṭinam, and Arikameḍu carried maritime trade. Guilds (śhreṇīs) — the Jātaka literature names eighteen kinds — regulated quality and prices and even worked as banks, as the Nāśhik cave inscription shows, recording donations deposited with guilds for interest.","visual":"⚓"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What fraction of produce, roughly, was the basic Mauryan land tax? (Answer as the denominator, e.g. for one-sixth answer 6)","answer":6,"explain":"The basic Mauryan land tax was generally a fixed proportion of the produce, about one-sixth."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which irrigation structure, built by the Chola king Karikala, remains in use even today?","options":["The Grand Anicut (Kallanai)","The Sudarshana Lake dam","The Nālandā tank","The Uttaramerur canal"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Grand Anicut, or Kallanai, was built by Karikala Chola and continues to be used and repaired to this day."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Jātaka literature, how many types of guilds (śhreṇīs) existed?","options":["Eighteen","Four","Fifty","Seven"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Jātaka literature refers to eighteen types of guilds, showing how well-established they were."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the Nāśhik cave inscription reveal about guilds?","options":["They functioned like banks, paying interest on deposited donations","They were purely religious organisations with no economic role","They were controlled directly by the king's treasury","They only accepted brāhmaṇa members"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Nāśhik cave inscription records donations deposited with guilds, which paid a fixed rate of interest, used to maintain Buddhist monasteries."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which two major land trade routes had emerged by the 6th century BCE?","options":["Dakṣhiṇāpatha and Uttarāpatha","The Silk Route and Spice Route","Grand Trunk Road and Kaveri Route","Sindhu Route and Ganga Route"],"answer":0,"explain":"By the 6th century BCE, Dakṣhiṇāpatha and Uttarāpatha had emerged as major land trade routes, later expanded by successive dynasties."}]},{"id":"ss-boss","title":"The Chronicler's Trial","icon":"📯","kind":"boss","story":"The path through a thousand years of dynasties, assemblies, and marketplaces has led you here — to the final gate, guarded by a spirit who demands proof that you truly understand how state and society took shape before 1000 CE.","boss":{"name":"The Keeper of the Saptāṁga","taunt":"Recite the seven limbs of the state, traveller, and name the dynasties that rose and fell — or your empire crumbles before you finish speaking!"},"teach":[{"title":"A Thousand Years in Review","body":"From kin-based janas and Vedic assemblies, to territorial janapadas and mahājanapadas, to empires like the Mauryas, Guptas, and Cholas — Indian state and society grew steadily more complex while holding onto enduring ideals like dharma and the chakravarti samrāṭ.","visual":"⌛"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who founded the Mauryan Empire in 321 BCE?","options":["Chandragupta Maurya","Aśhoka","Bindusāra","Puṣhyamitra Śhunga"],"answer":0,"explain":"Chandragupta Maurya founded the Mauryan Empire in 321 BCE, laying the base for one of early India's largest empires."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In which year BCE was the Mauryan Empire founded by Chandragupta Maurya?","answer":321,"explain":"The Mauryan Empire was founded in 321 BCE."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which text is one of the earliest and most systematic treatises on statecraft, attributed to Kauṭilya?","options":["The Arthaśhāstra","The Manu-smṛiti","The Nītisāra","The Amarakoṣha"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Arthaśhāstra is one of the earliest systematic treatises on statecraft, detailing administration and kingship."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What system did the Uttaramerur inscription describe for selecting Chola village assembly members?","options":["The Kudavolai (ballot-pot) system","The Saptāṁga system","The Pañchajana system","The Āśhrama system"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Kudavolai system used palm-leaf ballots drawn from a pot, in public, to select village assembly members."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which three powers fought the 'tri-partite struggle' to control Kannauj?","options":["Gurjara-Pratihāras, Pālas, and Rāṣhṭrakūṭas","Mauryas, Śhuṅgas, and Satavāhanas","Cholas, Cheras, and Pandyas","Guptas, Vākāṭakas, and Maitrakas"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Gurjara-Pratihāras, Pālas, and Rāṣhṭrakūṭas contested control of Kannauj in the tri-partite struggle."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the term janapada literally mean?","options":["Where a people (jana) first set its feet","The council of ministers","A fortified royal city","A trade guild's headquarters"],"answer":0,"explain":"Janapada literally means 'where a jana first set its feet', marking the shift to territorial identity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Gupta-period administrative post was newly introduced as the 'minister of peace and war'?","options":["Sāndhivigrahika","Pradeśhika","Grāmika","Amātya"],"answer":0,"explain":"The sāndhivigrahika was a new post introduced during the Gupta period to handle matters of peace and war."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In Kauṭilya's Saptāṁga, which term names 'the treasury or the wealth of the kingdom'?","options":["Kośha","Daṇḍa","Mitra","Durga"],"answer":0,"explain":"Kośha refers to the treasury or wealth of the kingdom, one of the seven Saptāṁga constituents."}]}]},{"id":"democracy9","index":5,"name":"Democracy","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 6","emoji":"🗳️","palette":{"from":"#4ade80","to":"#14532d"},"lessons":[{"id":"de1","title":"Roots of Indian Democracy","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into a time-portal that hums with the chants of ancient assemblies. Before you can understand modern India, you must trace the democratic spirit back through the Rigveda, the Buddha's monastic halls, and the Constituent Assembly's marathon debates.","teach":[{"title":"Vedic Assemblies","body":"From the earliest times, the Vedic king was never an independent ruler. He worked in consultation with assemblies such as the Sabha, Samiti, and Vidhata, and with the early republican states known as ganas or sanghas, all of which involved collective decision-making.","visual":"🕉️"},{"title":"The Aikyamatya Suktam","body":"A verse from the Aikyamatya Suktam of the Rigveda emphasises collective thinking, shared discussion, and unity of purpose. It shows that consultation, consensus, and shared responsibility have deep roots in India's intellectual and cultural traditions.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"The Bauddha Sangha","body":"The Bauddha Sangha, a monastic community established by Gautama Buddha, also reflects India's democratic traditions. It encouraged debate and discussion, and its members could choose their leader and make decisions through voting.","visual":"☮️"},{"title":"Colonial Disruption and Revival","body":"Invasions by foreign invaders disrupted the political and social fabric of parts of the country, and by the 19th century the British had colonised most of the Indian subcontinent, limiting people's participation in governance. However, the long freedom struggle revived and strengthened democratic ideas among the people.","visual":"⛓️"},{"title":"The Constituent Assembly","body":"Before independence in 1947, the Constituent Assembly was formed in 1946 to draft a constitution, taking 2 years, 11 months, and 18 days to produce the world's longest written Constitution. It was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950, and Article 368 allows it to be amended so it stays flexible and responsive.","visual":"📜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which three Vedic assemblies involved collective decision-making, working alongside the king?","options":["Sabha, Samiti, and Vidhata","Lok Sabha, Rajya Sabha, and Vidhan Sabha","Panchayat, Gram Sabha, and Municipality","Sangha, Sena, and Samiti"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter names the Sabha, Samiti, and Vidhata as Vedic assemblies of collective decision-making."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who established the Bauddha Sangha, where members debated issues and chose their leader through voting?","options":["Gautama Buddha","Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","Chaudhary Charan Singh","Jayaprakash Narayan"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Bauddha Sangha was a monastic community established by Gautama Buddha."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In what year did the Constitution of India come into force?","answer":1950,"explain":"The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Article of the Constitution provides for amendments, keeping it flexible and responsive?","options":["Article 368","Article 32","Article 21","Article 46"],"answer":0,"explain":"Article 368 provides for amendments to the Constitution."}]},{"id":"de2","title":"Popular Sovereignty and the Rule of Law","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"A mysterious ballot box appears in your path, guarded by a riddle: who really holds power here? To pass, you must master the twin pillars that keep any democracy honest -- the voice of the people and the rule of law.","teach":[{"title":"Popular Sovereignty","body":"In a democracy, the ultimate source of power lies with the people, meaning the state derives its authority from them. A government elected through free and fair elections formulates policies and implements laws on the people's behalf.","visual":"👥"},{"title":"Universal Adult Franchise","body":"Every citizen 18 years of age and above has the right to vote through a secret ballot. This right is known as the Universal Adult Franchise, and citizens use it to elect governments and representatives.","visual":"🗳️"},{"title":"The Rule of Law","body":"The Rule of Law ensures equality before the law and equal protection of the law, establishing that no one is above the law. When laws are violated, disputes are resolved in a court of law through established procedures, not by force or personal influence.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Procedure Established by Law","body":"No citizen can be punished without following the procedure established by law, and everyone gets a fair opportunity to defend their rights against arbitrary action. In this way, the rule of law promotes justice, accountability, and trust in the democratic system.","visual":"📜"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the idea called that the state derives its authority from the people?","options":["Popular Sovereignty","Federalism","Separation of Powers","Rule of Law"],"answer":0,"explain":"Popular sovereignty means the ultimate source of power lies with the people."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At what minimum age can an Indian citizen vote?","answer":18,"explain":"Every citizen 18 years of age and above has the right to vote, known as Universal Adult Franchise."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which principle ensures that no one, however powerful, is above the law?","options":["Rule of Law","Multi-Party System","Decentralisation","Popular Sovereignty"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Rule of Law establishes equality before the law and that no one is above it."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How do Indian citizens exercise their right to vote?","options":["Through a secret ballot","By a public show of hands","Through a written petition","By a panel of elders' decision"],"answer":0,"explain":"Citizens vote through a secret ballot as part of the Universal Adult Franchise."}]},{"id":"de3","title":"Fundamental Rights and Separation of Powers","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"Three great halls stand before you -- one for making laws, one for enforcing them, one for judging them. To cross safely you must learn why these halls were built apart, and what rights the Constitution locked away for every citizen.","teach":[{"title":"Six Fundamental Rights","body":"The Indian Constitution enshrines six Fundamental Rights: the Right to Equality (Articles 14-18), the Right to Freedom (19-22), the Right Against Exploitation (23-24), the Right to Freedom of Religion (25-28), Cultural and Educational Rights (29-30), and the Right to Constitutional Remedies (Article 32). These rights are enforceable in courts and are indivisible and inalienable, subject to reasonable restrictions.","visual":"📜"},{"title":"Constitutional Remedies","body":"Any violation of Fundamental Rights can lead to legal action, and citizens can seek constitutional remedies under Articles 32 and 226 for their protection and reinforcement.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Separation of Powers","body":"The legislature makes laws, the executive implements them, and the judiciary interprets them, each with distinct responsibilities. This separation prevents the concentration of power in any one organ and is essential for checks and balances.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"The Judiciary's Role","body":"The judiciary is an impartial, independent institution that upholds the Constitution, can review laws and amendments, and can declare a law unconstitutional if it is inconsistent with the Constitution. It has also used tools like Public Interest Litigation (PIL) to ensure access to justice for all.","visual":"🏛️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"How many Fundamental Rights are enshrined in the Indian Constitution?","answer":6,"explain":"The chapter lists six Fundamental Rights, from the Right to Equality to the Right to Constitutional Remedies."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which organ of government is responsible for interpreting laws?","options":["The Judiciary","The Legislature","The Executive","The Election Commission"],"answer":0,"explain":"Under separation of powers, the judiciary interprets laws, the legislature makes them, and the executive implements them."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Under which Article can citizens seek constitutional remedies for violation of their Fundamental Rights?","options":["Article 32","Article 368","Article 46","Article 21A"],"answer":0,"explain":"Article 32 provides the Right to Constitutional Remedies."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What legal tool has the judiciary used to ensure access to justice for all citizens?","options":["Public Interest Litigation (PIL)","Universal Adult Franchise","The RTI Act","The PESA Act"],"answer":0,"explain":"The judiciary has used Public Interest Litigation (PIL) to ensure access to justice for all."}]},{"id":"de4","title":"Accountability, Parties, and Protecting the Vulnerable","icon":"📜","kind":"lesson","story":"The town square buzzes with rival flags and shouting reporters. This is democracy at its noisiest -- parties competing for votes, journalists demanding answers, and a Constitution quietly making sure no community gets left behind.","teach":[{"title":"Accountability and Transparency","body":"Democratic governments are accountable to citizens through elections, public debates, and engagement with civil society. The Right to Information (RTI) Act, 2005, is a key example that allows citizens to seek information from government agencies.","visual":"📢"},{"title":"Multi-Party System","body":"In a multi-party system, several political parties contest elections, representing diverse voices and interests. The party or coalition that secures more than 50 per cent of the seats forms the government, while the largest opposing group becomes the opposition, all functioning under the Representation of the People Act, 1951.","visual":"🏳️"},{"title":"Safeguarding Vulnerable Groups","body":"It is the government's duty to protect all communities regardless of caste, gender, religion, or region. Article 46 of the Constitution states that the State shall promote with special care the educational and economic interests of the weaker sections, particularly the Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes.","visual":"🤝"},{"title":"Media, the Fourth Pillar","body":"Newspapers, news channels, and social media keep people informed and act as a voice of the masses, raising issues concerning the public. Owing to this critical role in safeguarding people's voices, media is often called the 'fourth pillar of democracy'.","visual":"📰"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Act, passed in 2005, allows citizens to seek information from government agencies?","options":["The Right to Information (RTI) Act","The Representation of the People Act","The PESA Act","The Right to Education Act"],"answer":0,"explain":"The RTI Act, 2005, allows citizens to seek information from government agencies."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In elections to Parliament or State Assemblies, what share of seats lets a party or coalition form the government?","options":["More than 50 per cent","Exactly 33 per cent","More than 75 per cent","Any single largest share, even under 20 per cent"],"answer":0,"explain":"The party or coalition securing more than 50 per cent of seats forms the government."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which institution is often referred to as the 'fourth pillar of democracy'?","options":["The Media","The Election Commission","The Judiciary","Civil Society"],"answer":0,"explain":"Media is called the fourth pillar of democracy for its role in safeguarding people's voices."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Article 46 of the Constitution directs the State to give special care to the educational and economic interests of which groups?","options":["Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes","Only the majority community","Only urban populations","Only government employees"],"answer":0,"explain":"Article 46 promotes the educational and economic interests of weaker sections, particularly SCs and STs."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Act lays down the rules and regulations for how political parties function in India?","options":["The Representation of the People Act, 1951","The RTI Act, 2005","The PESA Act, 1996","Article 368"],"answer":0,"explain":"Political parties function within the framework laid down by the Representation of the People Act, 1951."}]},{"id":"de5","title":"Types of Democracy Around the World","icon":"🌍","kind":"lesson","story":"A world map unfurls, glowing with four very different paths nations have taken to let their people rule. Learn to tell them apart before you can judge which model powers which country.","teach":[{"title":"Representative (Indirect) Democracy","body":"In a representative democracy, people elect their representatives rather than governing directly, through periodic elections, and the government remains accountable to the people. India is the example given for this type.","visual":"🗳️"},{"title":"Parliamentary Democracy","body":"In a parliamentary democracy, members of the Executive are also part of the legislature, and the Executive is accountable to the legislature. People elect the legislature but not the executive directly. India and Canada are given as examples.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Presidential Democracy","body":"In a presidential democracy, the Executive is independent of the legislature. The President is elected by the people and is accountable to them directly. The United States of America is the example given.","visual":"🏢"},{"title":"Direct Democracy","body":"In a direct democracy, citizens directly participate in most decision-making processes, though some features of representative democracy still exist. It is difficult to follow in large countries. Switzerland is the example given.","visual":"🗳️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which type of democracy do citizens directly participate in most decision-making processes?","options":["Direct Democracy","Presidential Democracy","Parliamentary Democracy","Representative Democracy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Direct Democracy involves citizens directly participating in most decisions, as practised in Switzerland."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which country is given as the example of a Presidential Democracy, where the Executive is independent of the legislature?","options":["United States of America","India","Canada","Switzerland"],"answer":0,"explain":"The USA is the chapter's example of a Presidential Democracy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a Parliamentary Democracy like India's, to whom is the Executive accountable?","options":["The legislature","The judiciary alone","The President only","Foreign governments"],"answer":0,"explain":"In a Parliamentary Democracy, the Executive is accountable to the legislature."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which country does the chapter give as the example of Direct Democracy?","options":["Switzerland","United Kingdom","United States of America","Canada"],"answer":0,"explain":"Switzerland is the chapter's example of Direct Democracy."}]},{"id":"de6","title":"India's Living Democracy and Its Challenges","icon":"🌱","kind":"lesson","story":"You descend from Parliament all the way down to a village Gram Sabha, where the true test of democracy happens every day. But the path is not without danger -- misinformation, inequality, and even a past Emergency threatened to dim its light.","teach":[{"title":"The Scale of Indian Democracy","body":"In 2024, India had over 96.8 crore registered voters, and on average a Member of Parliament represents about 25 lakh people (2019). Elections are conducted in 22 scheduled languages, with over one million polling stations and more than 2,800 registered political parties.","visual":"🗳️"},{"title":"Grassroots Democracy","body":"India's three-tier system of Union, State, and Local governments extends democracy to the grassroots through Panchayati Raj and Municipalities. The PESA Act, 1996, empowers the Gram Sabha as the primary decision-making body in Scheduled Areas, while Autonomous District Councils protect tribal customs in the north-east.","visual":"🏘️"},{"title":"Women and the Right to Vote","body":"In Britain, women got full voting rights only in 1928, and in the USA in 1920, after long struggles. In contrast, when India's Constitution came into force in 1950, Universal Adult Franchise was extended to men and women alike without any such struggle. Article 243(d) and 243(t) also reserve not less than one-third of seats for women in Panchayats and Municipalities.","visual":"🙋‍♀️"},{"title":"Challenges to Democracy","body":"Despite progress, Indian democracy faces challenges such as illiteracy, misinformation, and inequality. The spread of fake news through social media can influence public opinion and create confusion, while poverty, regionalism, and gender inequality create barriers to equal participation.","visual":"⚠️"},{"title":"The Emergency (1975-77)","body":"In June 1975, amid rising unemployment, inflation, and allegations of misgovernance, a National Emergency was imposed by Indira Gandhi's government. Fundamental Rights were suspended and the press was censored, until mass movements led by Jayaprakash Narayan (Lok Nayak) helped end it in 1977, when elections showed the resilience of Indian democracy.","visual":"⏳"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Act empowers the Gram Sabha as the primary decision-making body in tribal Scheduled Areas?","options":["The PESA Act, 1996","The RTI Act, 2005","The Representation of the People Act, 1951","Article 368"],"answer":0,"explain":"The PESA Act (Panchayats Extension to Scheduled Areas), 1996, empowers the Gram Sabha in Scheduled Areas."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In what year was the National Emergency imposed in India?","answer":1975,"explain":"The National Emergency was imposed in June 1975 and lasted until 1977."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who led the mass movement against the Emergency and was popularly known as Lok Nayak?","options":["Jayaprakash Narayan","Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","Indira Gandhi","Chaudhary Charan Singh"],"answer":0,"explain":"Jayaprakash Narayan, popularly known as Lok Nayak, led mass movements against the Emergency."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At minimum, what proportion of Panchayat seats is reserved for women under Article 243(d)?","options":["One-third","One-half","One-fourth","None"],"answer":0,"explain":"Article 243(d) provides not less than one-third reservation for women in Panchayats."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT listed in the chapter as a challenge facing Indian democracy today?","options":["Direct democracy","Illiteracy","Misinformation and fake news","Gender inequality"],"answer":0,"explain":"Direct democracy is a type of democracy, not a challenge; the chapter lists illiteracy, misinformation, and inequality as real challenges."}]},{"id":"de-boss","title":"Final Trial: Guardian of the Republic","icon":"👑","kind":"boss","story":"At the gates of the Republic stands a boss who feeds on apathy and silence, growing stronger whenever citizens stop questioning power. Only by proving your mastery of every democratic principle can you drive it back.","boss":{"name":"Autocratus, the Power-Hoarder","taunt":"Why share power with the people when one throne is so much simpler?"},"teach":[],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a democracy, where does the ultimate source of power and authority lie?","options":["With the people","With the military","With the monarch","With the bureaucracy"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ultimate source of power in a democracy lies with the people, who elect their representatives."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In which year did the Constitution of India come into force?","answer":1950,"explain":"The Constitution was adopted on 26 November 1949 and came into force on 26 January 1950."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which principle ensures that everyone, including the powerful, is equally subject to the law?","options":["Rule of Law","Federalism","Sovereignty","Multi-Party System"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Rule of Law establishes that no one is above the law and everyone gets equal protection."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which type of democracy does Switzerland practise, where citizens directly participate in most decisions?","options":["Direct Democracy","Presidential Democracy","Parliamentary Democracy","Representative Democracy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Switzerland is the chapter's example of Direct Democracy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At minimum, what share of seats must be reserved for women in Panchayats under Article 243(d)?","options":["One-third","One-half","One-fourth","None"],"answer":0,"explain":"Article 243(d) guarantees not less than one-third reservation for women in Panchayats."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which 1975-77 event saw Fundamental Rights suspended and the press censored in India?","options":["The Emergency","The Constituent Assembly Debates","The RTI movement","The drafting of Article 368"],"answer":0,"explain":"The National Emergency of 1975-77 saw Fundamental Rights suspended and the press censored."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which institution does the chapter describe as the 'fourth pillar of democracy'?","options":["Media","Judiciary","Election Commission","Civil Society"],"answer":0,"explain":"Media is called the fourth pillar of democracy for its role in informing and voicing public concerns."}]}]},{"id":"elections9","index":6,"name":"Elections","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 7","emoji":"🗳️","palette":{"from":"#f87171","to":"#7f1d1d"},"lessons":[{"id":"el1","title":"Why Elections Matter","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"You step into the Democracy Arena, where every five years the people summon their representatives through the ballot. A voice echoes: 'No vote lasts forever, traveller -- power must be renewed, or it turns to dust.' Your quest begins with the question every citizen must answer: why do we vote again and again?","teach":[{"title":"Direct vs Indirect Elections","body":"In India, members of the Lok Sabha, the Vidhan Sabha (Legislative Assembly), and local bodies like Panchayats and municipal corporations are chosen through direct elections held every five years. The President, Vice President, and members of the Rajya Sabha, in contrast, are chosen through indirect elections.","visual":"🗳️"},{"title":"Periodic Elections and Accountability","body":"A representative cannot continue in office indefinitely without facing a fresh election, and a government cannot continue beyond its prescribed term without seeking the people's mandate again. Elected representatives are accountable to the people, and our vote is the principal means by which we exercise the right to elect them and hold them accountable.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"What Elections Ensure","body":"Elections matter for a democracy because they secure Equality (every citizen gets an equal voice), Representation (people choose who speaks for them), Accountability (representatives answer to voters), and Legitimacy (the government's authority comes from the people).","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Psephology","body":"Psephology is the scientific study of elections. The word comes from the Greek 'psephos' (pebble) and 'logy' (systematic study), because in ancient Greece pebbles were used to cast votes.","visual":"🪨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is chosen through a DIRECT election in India?","options":["Members of the Lok Sabha","The President of India","The Vice President of India","Members of the Rajya Sabha"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lok Sabha, Vidhan Sabha, and local body members are directly elected by citizens every five years."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is chosen through an INDIRECT election?","options":["The President of India","A Lok Sabha MP","A Vidhan Sabha MLA","A Panchayat member"],"answer":0,"explain":"The President, Vice President, and Rajya Sabha members are elected indirectly, not by direct public vote."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How often are direct elections to the Lok Sabha, Vidhan Sabha, and local bodies held?","options":["Every five years","Every four years","Every six years","Every ten years"],"answer":0,"explain":"Direct elections to these bodies are held every five years, unless the body is dissolved earlier."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the term 'psephology' mean?","options":["The scientific study of elections","The study of political parties only","The process of counting ballots by hand","The study of constitutional amendments"],"answer":0,"explain":"Psephology comes from the Greek word for 'pebble' plus 'logy' (study), referring to the scientific study of elections."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why can't an elected representative simply continue in office without a fresh election, once their term ends?","options":["Because representatives must be held accountable through periodic elections","Because the Constitution has no rule about terms","Because only the ECI can decide who stays in office","Because elections are optional in a democracy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Periodic elections and the possibility of being voted out are what make elected representatives accountable to the people."}]},{"id":"el2","title":"The Electoral System","icon":"⚙️","kind":"lesson","story":"Deep in the Constitution's engine room, you find the machine that turns millions of votes into seats of power. Two great designs lie before you -- one crowns whoever gets the most votes, the other shares power in proportion to support. Choose wisely: India's framers already did, and you must learn why.","teach":[{"title":"First-Past-The-Post (FPTP)","body":"The framers of the Constitution chose the plurality system, also called First-Past-The-Post (FPTP), for elections to the Lok Sabha and the Vidhan Sabha. Under FPTP, each voter votes for one candidate in their constituency, and the candidate with the most votes wins, even if that is less than 50% of the votes polled.","visual":"🏁"},{"title":"Proportional Representation","body":"Elections to the Rajya Sabha, and elections of the President and Vice President, are conducted using the system of proportional representation, where seats or influence are distributed in proportion to votes received rather than going only to the single top candidate.","visual":"📊"},{"title":"The Vidhan Parishad","body":"Six states -- Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Karnataka, Maharashtra, Telangana, and Uttar Pradesh -- have a bicameral legislature with two houses: the Vidhan Sabha (Legislative Assembly, the lower house) and the Vidhan Parishad (Legislative Council, the upper house). The Vidhan Parishad is elected by MLAs, local bodies, graduates, and teachers, with some members nominated by the Governor.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Single Transferable Vote (STV)","body":"Elections to the Vidhan Parishad use proportional representation by single transferable vote. Voters mark their preferences; candidates who reach the quota (total valid votes divided by seats-to-be-filled-plus-one, plus one) are elected, and the lowest-scoring candidate is eliminated with their votes transferred by second preference, until all seats are filled.","visual":"🔁"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which electoral system is used to elect members of the Lok Sabha and the Vidhan Sabha?","options":["First-Past-The-Post (FPTP)","Proportional Representation","Single Transferable Vote alone","Majority System"],"answer":0,"explain":"India's Constitution framers chose the plurality or First-Past-The-Post system for Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha elections."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these states has a Vidhan Parishad (a bicameral legislature)?","options":["Karnataka","Rajasthan","Punjab","Odisha"],"answer":0,"explain":"Karnataka is one of the six states -- along with Andhra Pradesh, Bihar, Maharashtra, Telangana, and Uttar Pradesh -- with a Vidhan Parishad."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How is the Vidhan Parishad elected?","options":["Proportional representation by single transferable vote","First-Past-The-Post","Direct election by all citizens","Nomination by the Prime Minister"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Vidhan Parishad is elected through proportional representation using the single transferable vote system."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Under STV, the quota is (total valid votes / (seats to be filled + 1)) + 1. If there are 60,000 total valid votes and 5 seats to be filled, what is the quota?","answer":10001,"explain":"60,000 / (5+1) = 10,000, and adding 1 gives a quota of 10,001 votes to win a seat."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Under FPTP, must a candidate win more than 50% of the votes to be declared the winner?","options":["No, the candidate with the most votes wins even if it is less than 50%","Yes, more than 50% is always required","Only if the seat is uncontested","Only in Vidhan Sabha elections, not Lok Sabha"],"answer":0,"explain":"FPTP declares the candidate with the most votes the winner, regardless of whether they cross 50%."}]},{"id":"el3","title":"The Laws Behind the Ballot","icon":"📜","kind":"lesson","story":"Two ancient scrolls guard the gates of India's electoral fortress, each inscribed in 1950 and 1951. Without them, no roll of voters would exist and no rule would stop a candidate from cheating. Read closely -- the guardians of fairness are hidden in the fine print.","teach":[{"title":"Representation of the People Act, 1950","body":"RPA 1950 deals mainly with the allocation of seats and delimitation of constituencies, the preparation and revision of electoral rolls, and ensuring that every citizen of 18 years of age or above has the right to vote without discrimination.","visual":"📋"},{"title":"Representation of the People Act, 1951","body":"RPA 1951 deals with the conduct of elections and post-election disputes -- laying down rules for nomination of candidates, election campaigns, voting procedures, and dispute resolution. It also lists electoral offences and corrupt practices, such as bribing voters, appealing for votes on the basis of religion, caste, race, community, or language, and taking help from government personnel like magistrates or police.","visual":"⛔"},{"title":"The Delimitation Commission","body":"Delimitation is the process of fixing the number of seats and territorial boundaries of constituencies for the Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha, so that the ratio of seats to population stays as equal as possible. Article 82 of the Constitution mandates a Delimitation Commission; India has had four so far, in 1952, 1963, 1973, and 2002.","visual":"🗺️"},{"title":"Presidential and Vice-Presidential Elections Act, 1952","body":"Alongside RPA 1950 and 1951, the Presidential and Vice-Presidential Elections Act, 1952 specifically governs the conduct of elections to the offices of President and Vice President of India.","visual":"🖋️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Act deals with the preparation of electoral rolls and the right of every citizen above 18 to vote without discrimination?","options":["Representation of the People Act, 1950","Representation of the People Act, 1951","Presidential and Vice-Presidential Elections Act, 1952","Anti-Defection Law, 1985"],"answer":0,"explain":"RPA 1950 covers seat allocation, delimitation, and preparation/revision of electoral rolls."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which Act lays down rules for candidate nomination, election campaigns, and resolution of post-election disputes?","options":["Representation of the People Act, 1951","Representation of the People Act, 1950","Article 82 of the Constitution","The Voters' Pledge"],"answer":0,"explain":"RPA 1951 governs the conduct of elections and post-election disputes, including nomination and campaigning rules."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the main purpose of the Delimitation Commission?","options":["To fix constituency boundaries so the seat-to-population ratio stays roughly equal","To register political parties","To print ballot papers","To count votes on election day"],"answer":0,"explain":"Delimitation determines the number of seats and territorial boundaries so no constituency is wildly over- or under-represented."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"As of the years listed in the chapter, how many Delimitation Commissions has India had?","answer":4,"explain":"India has had four Delimitation Commissions, formed in 1952, 1963, 1973, and 2002."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is listed as an electoral corrupt practice under RPA 1951?","options":["Appealing for votes on the basis of religion, caste, or language","Distributing election manifestos","Holding a public campaign rally","Putting up campaign posters"],"answer":0,"explain":"RPA 1951 bans appeals for votes made on the grounds of religion, caste, race, community, or language."}]},{"id":"el4","title":"Election Commission of India","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"You arrive at the command center of the Election Commission of India, the guardian entrusted by the Constitution with the 'superintendence, direction, and control' of every election. From electoral rolls to Braille EVMs, its machinery must serve over 96.8 crore voters without missing a single one.","teach":[{"title":"An Autonomous Constitutional Body","body":"The ECI is an autonomous permanent constitutional body established on 25 January 1950. Articles 324 to 329 of the Constitution provide for its establishment and define its powers and duties. It conducts elections to the Lok Sabha, Rajya Sabha, Vidhan Sabha, Vidhan Parishad, President, and Vice President.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Electoral Rolls and Special Intensive Revision","body":"The ECI sends enumerators to every household to prepare electoral rolls, organised polling-station-wise, so only listed citizens can vote. Through Special Intensive Revision (SIR), it updates, verifies, and corrects these rolls -- adding newly-eligible young voters and deleting names of the deceased, those who moved, duplicates, and the permanently untraceable.","visual":"📋"},{"title":"Scheduling, Registering Parties, and Symbols","body":"When a Legislature's five-year term ends or it is dissolved early, the ECI decides the election schedule, weighing factors like weather, agriculture, exams, and festivals. It also registers political parties, allocates election symbols, classifies parties as national, state, or Registered-Unrecognised (RUPP), and acts as a quasi-judicial body in symbol and recognition disputes.","visual":"📅"},{"title":"Making Elections Inclusive","body":"The ECI runs a digital ecosystem for voters and candidates: ETPBS lets service voters cast postal ballots electronically from outside their constituency, the cVIGIL app lets citizens report Model Code of Conduct violations, and the Saksham app assists persons with disabilities. Since the 2024 General Elections, senior citizens above 85 and PwDs with 40%+ disability can vote from home.","visual":"📱"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When was the Election Commission of India established?","options":["25 January 1950","26 January 1950","15 August 1947","26 November 1949"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ECI was established as an autonomous constitutional body on 25 January 1950."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does SIR (an ECI initiative) stand for?","options":["Special Intensive Revision","State Identity Record","Secure Integrated Registration","Statutory Inclusion Report"],"answer":0,"explain":"SIR stands for Special Intensive Revision, which updates, verifies, and corrects the electoral rolls."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which app lets voters report Model Code of Conduct violations so flying squads can respond quickly?","options":["cVIGIL","Voter Helpline","Suvidha","Sugam"],"answer":0,"explain":"The cVIGIL app lets citizens report violations within minutes, guiding flying squads to the location."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does ETPBS enable service voters to do?","options":["Receive and cast postal ballots electronically from outside their constituency","Vote only at their home polling booth","Register as a new voter online","Report election malpractice"],"answer":0,"explain":"ETPBS (Electronically Transmitted Postal Ballot System) lets service voters vote electronically from wherever they are posted."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Since the 2024 General Elections, voting from home has been extended to senior citizens above what age?","answer":85,"explain":"Senior citizens above 85 years of age, and PwDs with 40%+ benchmark disability, were extended home voting in 2024."}]},{"id":"el5","title":"Political Parties in the Arena","icon":"🤝","kind":"lesson","story":"Banners rise across the land as party symbols march into the arena -- lotus, hand, broom, and more. Some warriors switch banners mid-battle, and an ancient law watches for betrayal. Learn who may fight under which flag, and what happens when loyalty breaks.","teach":[{"title":"Why Political Parties Matter","body":"Voters learn about candidates, their policies, and their positions on social, economic, cultural, and political issues mainly through political parties. Parties enable informed decisions, organise public opinion, campaign on issues, and offer voters meaningful choices, helping democracy function through governance, accountability, representation, and public engagement.","visual":"📣"},{"title":"India's Multi-Party System","body":"India has a multi-party system, meaning many political parties exist and compete in elections, reflecting the country's diversity of languages, cultures, religions, and regions.","visual":"🏳️"},{"title":"Defection and the Anti-Defection Law","body":"Defection means abandoning or switching from the party under whose banner a candidate was elected, including acting independently of the party's decisions such as voting against it. The Anti-Defection Law, passed in 1985 through the 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act, allows disqualification of an MP or MLA who voluntarily gives up party membership or defies the party whip; the Speaker or Chairman of the House decides such cases.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Recognising National, State, and RUPP Parties","body":"The ECI recognises a party as a National Party if it wins 6% of valid votes in 4+ states plus 4 Lok Sabha seats (or meets similar alternative conditions), and as a State Party based on vote-share or seat thresholds within one state. Parties that have not secured enough votes, or have never contested since registration, are called Registered Unrecognised Political Parties (RUPP).","visual":"📝"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which year was the Anti-Defection Law passed, and by which amendment?","options":["1985, through the 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act","1975, through the 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act","1991, through the 61st Constitutional Amendment Act","1950, through the First Amendment Act"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Anti-Defection Law was passed in 1985 through the 52nd Constitutional Amendment Act."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is 'defection' as described in the chapter?","options":["Abandoning or switching from the party under whose banner a candidate was elected","Losing an election as an independent candidate","Registering a new political party","Campaigning for another candidate in your own party"],"answer":0,"explain":"Defection means leaving or acting against the party under whose banner one was originally elected."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Who decides disqualification cases under the Anti-Defection Law?","options":["The Speaker or Chairman of the House","The Election Commission of India","The Supreme Court of India","The President of India"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Speaker or Chairman of the respective House decides what action to take in defection cases."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"To be recognised as a National Party (under one criterion), a party must secure at least what percentage of valid votes in four or more states, along with winning 4 Lok Sabha seats?","options":["6%","3%","8%","10%"],"answer":0,"explain":"One path to National Party recognition is securing at least 6% of valid votes in 4+ states plus 4 Lok Sabha seats."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are parties called that are registered with the ECI but have not secured enough votes, or have never contested an election since registering?","options":["Registered Unrecognised Political Parties (RUPP)","State Parties","National Parties","Independent Alliances"],"answer":0,"explain":"Such parties are classified as Registered Unrecognised Political Parties, or RUPP."}]},{"id":"el6","title":"Challenges to Free and Fair Elections","icon":"🚧","kind":"lesson","story":"The road to free and fair elections is pitted with potholes -- misinformation, fake news, and intimidation lie in wait for over 96.8 crore voters. Arm yourself with the ECI's toolkit: laws, machines, and paper trails, and see how vigilance keeps democracy from swerving off course.","teach":[{"title":"A Massive, Diverse Task","body":"Conducting elections for over 96.8 crore eligible voters (in 2024), across thousands of polling stations and hundreds of political parties, spread over diverse regions and socio-economic realities, is a huge challenge for the ECI.","visual":"🌍"},{"title":"Potholes on the Road","body":"The chapter highlights challenges such as misinformation, fake news, and intimidation as 'potholes' on the road to free and fair elections that can undermine the democratic value of the process.","visual":"🕳️"},{"title":"Tools That Safeguard Fairness","body":"To address these challenges, the ECI relies on RPA 1950 and 1951, the Model Code of Conduct, Electronic Voting Machines (EVMs), the Voter Verifiable Paper Audit Trail (VVPAT), and voters' awareness campaigns.","visual":"🗳️"},{"title":"Democracy Is More Than Voting","body":"If votes are miscounted, candidates use undue influence, or some voters face accessibility barriers, elections lose democratic value even if they are held. Democracy requires the process to be inclusive, impartial, and trustworthy -- sustained by constant vigilance and active citizen participation.","visual":"🤝"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which three challenges are shown as 'potholes' on the road to free and fair elections in the chapter?","options":["Misinformation, Fake News, Intimidation","Delimitation, Defection, Symbols","EVMs, VVPAT, ETPBS","RPA 1950, RPA 1951, Article 82"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter's Fig. 7.13 shows misinformation, fake news, and intimidation as challenges on the road to fair elections."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tool lets a voter verify that their EVM vote was recorded correctly?","options":["Voter Verifiable Paper Audit Trail (VVPAT)","cVIGIL","ETPBS","Suvidha"],"answer":0,"explain":"VVPAT gives voters a paper slip confirmation that their vote was cast as intended."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Roughly how many crore eligible voters did India have in the 2024 elections, according to the chapter?","answer":96.8,"explain":"The chapter states India had over 96.8 crore eligible voters in 2024."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What guideline regulates candidate and party conduct during campaigns to help prevent violations like misinformation and intimidation?","options":["Model Code of Conduct","Anti-Defection Law","Delimitation Act","Voters' Pledge"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Model Code of Conduct is one of the tools the ECI uses to keep campaigning fair."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the chapter, is democracy limited only to the act of holding elections?","options":["No, it also requires the process to be inclusive, impartial, and trustworthy","Yes, holding an election is enough regardless of fairness","Yes, as long as an EVM is used","No, only the vote count matters"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter stresses that democracy needs elections to be inclusive, impartial, and trustworthy, not just held."}]},{"id":"el-boss","title":"The Commissioner's Challenge","icon":"🛡️","kind":"boss","story":"At the heart of the Democracy Arena stands the final guardian -- the Commissioner's Challenge -- testing everything you have learned about elections, laws, and the machinery that keeps India's democracy free and fair.","boss":{"name":"The Ballot Guardian","taunt":"Millions of voters trust this system -- prove you understand it, or your victory is disqualified!"},"teach":[{"title":"Final Review","body":"Recall the whole journey: why elections matter, the FPTP and proportional representation systems, the RPA 1950 and 1951, the Delimitation Commission, the ECI's many functions, the role of political parties, and the challenges elections face in practice.","visual":"🧩"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which electoral system is used to elect members of the Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha?","options":["First-Past-The-Post (FPTP)","Proportional Representation","Single Transferable Vote","Majority System with runoff"],"answer":0,"explain":"The plurality or FPTP system, where the candidate with the most votes wins, is used for Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabha elections."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is elected through an INDIRECT election?","options":["The President of India","A Lok Sabha MP","A Vidhan Sabha MLA","A municipal corporation member"],"answer":0,"explain":"The President, Vice President, and Rajya Sabha members are chosen through indirect elections."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which constitutional body has the 'superintendence, direction, and control' of the entire process of conducting elections in India?","options":["Election Commission of India (ECI)","Delimitation Commission","Supreme Court of India","Ministry of Home Affairs"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Constitution vests the ECI with superintendence, direction, and control of the conduct of elections."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"Under STV, quota = (total valid votes / (seats to be filled + 1)) + 1. With 40,000 total valid votes and 3 seats to be filled, what is the quota?","answer":10001,"explain":"40,000 / (3+1) = 10,000, plus 1 gives a quota of 10,001 votes."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which law allows disqualification of an MP or MLA who voluntarily leaves their party or defies the party whip?","options":["The Anti-Defection Law (1985)","Representation of the People Act, 1950","Delimitation Act","Presidential and Vice-Presidential Elections Act, 1952"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Anti-Defection Law, passed via the 52nd Constitutional Amendment in 1985, governs disqualification for defection."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which tool lets a voter confirm their EVM vote was correctly recorded?","options":["Voter Verifiable Paper Audit Trail (VVPAT)","cVIGIL","Saksham App","Sugam"],"answer":0,"explain":"VVPAT provides a paper-trail confirmation of each EVM vote cast."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is the scientific study of elections called?","options":["Psephology","Sociology","Delimitation","Demography"],"answer":0,"explain":"Psephology, from the Greek 'psephos' (pebble) and 'logy' (study), is the scientific study of elections."}]}]},{"id":"choiceeconomics9","index":7,"name":"The Problem of Choice","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 8 — Building Blocks in Economics","emoji":"⚖️","palette":{"from":"#2dd4bf","to":"#134e4a"},"lessons":[{"id":"pc1","title":"Needs, Wants and the Problem of Choice","icon":"🧭","kind":"lesson","story":"Your pocket money won't stretch to both snacks and new shoes, and your farmer neighbour can't grow unlimited barley and wheat on the same field. Welcome to the very first puzzle of economics: everyone, everywhere, must choose.","teach":[{"title":"Needs vs Wants","body":"Needs are essentials like food, water, and shelter. Wants are things like gadgets, vacations, or luxury items — desirable but not essential. Human wants are unlimited and keep changing; people may want to upgrade from a bicycle to a motorbike, and then to a car.","visual":"🍞"},{"title":"Resources Are Limited","body":"Resources — the factors of production (land, labour, capital, and technology) — are needed to satisfy wants, but both natural and human-made resources are limited in quantity. They can be put to many alternative uses, such as steel being used for medical equipment, refrigerators, or aircraft manufacturing.","visual":"🔩"},{"title":"Opportunity Cost","body":"When one alternative is chosen, the other options are given up. The value of what is given up is known as the opportunity cost — the true price of every choice you make.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"The Production Possibility Curve","body":"Imagine a farmer choosing how much barley and wheat to grow on a fixed piece of land. Plotting all the possible combinations gives a downward-sloping Production Possibility Curve (PPC) — growing more barley means sacrificing some wheat. Every point on the curve shows the maximum output possible using resources efficiently, without wastage.","visual":"📈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A family spends money on food, medicines, and school supplies. According to the chapter, these are examples of:","options":["Needs","Wants","Resources","Opportunity costs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Needs are essentials like food, water, shelter, and medicines — as opposed to wants like gadgets, vacations, or jewellery."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What is 'opportunity cost'?","options":["The value of the next best alternative given up when a choice is made","The total money spent while shopping","The price fixed by the government for a good","The cost of transporting goods to market"],"answer":0,"explain":"When one alternative is chosen, the value of the option given up is the opportunity cost."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"On the farmer's Production Possibility Curve, moving from point A to point E means the farmer produces:","options":["More barley and less wheat","More wheat and less barley","More of both crops","Less of both crops"],"answer":0,"explain":"As the farmer moves from A to E, barley output rises while wheat output falls — wheat sacrificed is the opportunity cost of growing more barley."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the farmer's Production Possibility table, Combination C is 50 kg of barley. How many kg of wheat does Combination C produce?","answer":70,"explain":"The chapter's table lists Combination C as 50 kg barley and 70 kg wheat."}]},{"id":"pc2","title":"What Does Economics Deal With?","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"Long before stock markets and budgets existed, the Greeks had a word for managing a household wisely. Today that same idea has grown to guide entire nations — and the annual Economic Survey is where India writes its own report card.","teach":[{"title":"The Word 'Economics'","body":"Economics comes from the Greek word oikonomia, made up of oikos ('household') and nemein ('management') — literally, household management. Today, not just families but nations must plan how to use their limited resources efficiently.","visual":"🏺"},{"title":"What Economics Studies","body":"Economics deals with how choices are made by optimising the use of limited resources to satisfy needs and wants. It explains how consumers, producers, governments, and financial institutions interact — how people earn wages, how prices are determined, and how government policies influence the economy.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"The Scope of an Economist's Work","body":"Economists work across many fields: policy-making (guiding governments on taxation or welfare spending), finance (advising investors), research and education (studying trends and teaching), and business consulting (helping firms plan growth or improve efficiency).","visual":"💼"},{"title":"The Economic Survey of India","body":"The Economic Survey of India is an important annual document prepared by the Ministry of Finance and presented in Parliament before the Union Budget. It reviews the country's economic performance and acts as a blueprint for the upcoming Union Budget.","visual":"📑"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The word 'economics' comes from the Greek oikonomia, meaning:","options":["Household management","Market trading","Government planning","Resource extraction"],"answer":0,"explain":"Oikonomia combines oikos ('household') and nemein ('management') — household management."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is NOT part of the scope of an economist's work described in the chapter?","options":["Weather forecasting","Policy-making","Business consulting","Research and education"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter lists policy-making, finance, research and education, and business consulting as the scope of an economist's work — not weather forecasting."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Economic Survey of India is prepared by:","options":["The Ministry of Finance","The Reserve Bank of India","The Planning Commission","The Ministry of Education"],"answer":0,"explain":"It is prepared by the Ministry of Finance and presented in Parliament before the Union Budget."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What role does the Economic Survey play with respect to the Union Budget?","options":["It acts as a blueprint, providing crucial insights for policymakers","It replaces the need for a budget altogether","It is written after the budget is passed","It has no connection to the budget"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Survey is presented before the Union Budget and acts as a blueprint for it."}]},{"id":"pc3","title":"The Three Big Questions","icon":"❓","kind":"lesson","story":"Scarcity is a strict examiner — every economy on Earth must sit the same three-question test: what to produce, how to produce, and for whom. Get ready to see how a pair of shoes answers all three.","teach":[{"title":"Scarcity Creates Choices","body":"Any mismatch between unlimited wants and limited resources creates scarcity, which forces choices. This gives rise to three key questions that economics seeks to address: what to produce, how to produce, and for whom to produce.","visual":"⚡"},{"title":"What to Produce?","body":"This question concerns which goods and services, and in what quantities, should be produced. For example, farmers could grow water-intensive sugarcane, which yields high profit, or drought-resistant millets, which save water and improve soil health — the opportunity cost of growing sugarcane is the forgone gains from saved water and better soil.","visual":"🌾"},{"title":"For Whom to Produce?","body":"This question asks who benefits from what is produced. Shoes show this well: school shoes are simple, durable, and affordable for students; office-wear shoes focus on comfort and formal appearance; sports shoes give athletes grip and flexibility; and casual shoes offer affordable daily comfort.","visual":"👟"},{"title":"How to Produce?","body":"This question is about which methods and resources should be used — labour-intensive production (more workers, less machinery) or capital-intensive production (more machines and technology, fewer workers). Agriculture and handicrafts tend to rely more on labour, while industries like steel and automobiles depend more on machinery.","visual":"🏭"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Scarcity arises mainly because:","options":["Human wants are unlimited but resources are limited","Governments regulate too many markets","Prices keep changing every day","Farmers grow only one type of crop"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mismatch between unlimited wants and limited resources is the root of scarcity."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A farmer chooses to grow sugarcane instead of millets. What is the opportunity cost of this choice, according to the chapter?","options":["The forgone gains from saved water and improved soil health","The market price of sugarcane","The cost of fertiliser used","The wages paid to farm labourers"],"answer":0,"explain":"Growing sugarcane means giving up the water savings and soil health benefits that growing millets would have provided."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Sports shoes are designed with special rubber soles and lightweight materials for athletes. This is an example of producers answering which key question?","options":["For whom to produce","What to produce","How to produce","Why to produce"],"answer":0,"explain":"Different shoe types are made for different groups of consumers based on their needs — this answers 'for whom to produce'."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A garment manufacturer switches from hand-stitching to automated machines because machines have become cheaper. This decision mainly answers the question of:","options":["How to produce","What to produce","For whom to produce","Where to produce"],"answer":0,"explain":"Choosing between labour-intensive and capital-intensive methods answers 'how to produce'."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the chapter, which economic activities typically rely MORE on labour rather than machinery?","options":["Agriculture and handicrafts","Steel manufacturing","Automobile manufacturing","Aircraft manufacturing"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states agriculture and handicrafts rely more on labour, while steel and automobiles depend more on machinery."}]},{"id":"pc4","title":"Economic Systems","icon":"🌍","kind":"lesson","story":"Three very different kingdoms answer the same three questions in three different ways — one ruled by a central planner, one ruled by the marketplace, and one where both share the throne. India itself has lived in more than one of these kingdoms.","teach":[{"title":"Planned Economy","body":"In a planned economy, a central planning authority of the government makes all major economic decisions — what, how, and for whom to produce. The government owns most resources like land, factories, and banks. Examples include the former Soviet Union, North Korea, and Cuba.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Market Economy","body":"In a market economy, decisions are driven mainly by the forces of demand and supply, with little government intervention — the government acts like a referee in a football match, ensuring law and order without controlling prices. Ownership rests largely with individuals and private companies. Examples include the USA, Japan, and Hong Kong.","visual":"🤝"},{"title":"Mixed Economy","body":"A mixed economy combines features of both market and planned economies — private individuals, enterprises, and the government all play roles in economic decisions, alongside large public sector companies. Almost all real-world economies are mixed. Examples include India (post-1991), China (post-1978), Germany, and Sweden.","visual":"⚙️"},{"title":"India's Economic Journey","body":"After Independence, India followed a state-led approach similar to a planned economy, with the government controlling industries through licenses and permits. By 1991, economic difficulties led to major reforms that reduced regulation, encouraged private enterprise, and opened the economy to global trade — shifting India toward a more market-oriented mixed economy.","visual":"🇮🇳"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In which economic system does a central planning authority make most major decisions on what, how, and for whom to produce?","options":["Planned economy","Market economy","Mixed economy","Informal economy"],"answer":0,"explain":"In a planned economy, a central authority such as a planning commission makes these decisions."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"In a market economy, the chapter compares the government's role to:","options":["A referee in a football match","A factory owner","A central planner","A shopkeeper"],"answer":0,"explain":"The government ensures safety and law and order without controlling prices or production, much like a referee."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these is given as an example of a mixed economy in the chapter?","options":["India (post-1991)","North Korea","Cuba","the former Soviet Union"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter lists India (post-1991), China (post-1978), Germany, and Sweden as examples of mixed economies."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What major change did India introduce around 1991?","options":["Reforms that reduced regulation and opened the economy to global trade","A shift to a fully planned economy","The complete abolition of private enterprise","The removal of all government involvement in the economy"],"answer":0,"explain":"Facing economic difficulties, India introduced reforms encouraging private enterprise and global trade, moving toward a mixed economy."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the chapter, which statement about real-world economies is true?","options":["Almost all economies are mixed, combining private ownership with government regulation","Pure market economies have no government involvement at all","Pure planned economies are the most common today","Mixed economies never include public sector companies"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter notes that most economies have features of mixed economic systems, and even strong market economies like the USA have significant government involvement."}]},{"id":"pc-boss","title":"The Scarcity Sphinx","icon":"🐉","kind":"boss","story":"Resources are limited, wants are endless, and the Scarcity Sphinx blocks the path — answer its riddles on needs, choices, and economic systems, or be trapped forever between a notebook and a tennis racket.","boss":{"name":"The Scarcity Sphinx","taunt":"Resources are limited, wants are endless — prove you understand the trade-offs, or be lost to indecision forever!"},"teach":[{"title":"Review: The Big Idea","body":"Economics is about making choices in a world of scarce resources and unlimited wants. Every choice involves an opportunity cost — giving up one option for another. Every economy faces three key questions: what, how, and for whom to produce, and different economic systems — market, planned, and mixed — answer them differently.","visual":"🧠"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A student has ₹100 and must choose between buying a notebook or saving the money to buy a tennis racket later. Which economic concept best explains this situation?","options":["Opportunity cost","Demand","Production","Inflation"],"answer":0,"explain":"Choosing one option means giving up the other — the essence of opportunity cost."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What are the three key questions every economy must answer?","options":["What to produce, how to produce, for whom to produce","What to buy, when to buy, where to buy","Who earns, who spends, who saves","What is scarce, what is costly, what is cheap"],"answer":0,"explain":"These three questions arise from the mismatch between unlimited wants and limited resources."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which economic system relies mainly on demand and supply, with limited government intervention?","options":["Market economy","Planned economy","Mixed economy","None of the above"],"answer":0,"explain":"In a market economy, prices and production are determined primarily by market forces."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"On the farmer's Production Possibility Curve, Combination D is 75 kg of barley. How many kg of wheat does it produce?","answer":40,"explain":"The chapter's table lists Combination D as 75 kg barley and 40 kg wheat."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why do all points on the Production Possibility Curve represent the maximum possible output?","options":["Because they reflect efficient use of all available resources without wastage","Because they show the cheapest combination to produce","Because they represent government-set production targets","Because they show the minimum resources needed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter states that points on the PPC show maximum output through efficient use of resources by avoiding wastage."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Which of these best distinguishes a 'need' from a 'want'?","options":["Needs are essentials like food and shelter; wants are desirable but not essential, like gadgets or vacations","Needs are bought only with savings; wants are bought only with loans","Needs are produced by the government; wants are produced only by private firms","Needs are unlimited; wants are limited"],"answer":0,"explain":"Needs are essentials like food, water, and shelter, while wants are desirable but non-essential items like gadgets or vacations."}]}]},{"id":"pricepuzzle9","index":8,"name":"The Price Puzzle","subtitle":"Understanding Society Chapter 9 — What Drives the Market","emoji":"💰","palette":{"from":"#818cf8","to":"#312e81"},"lessons":[{"id":"pp1","title":"The Law of Demand","icon":"🥭","kind":"lesson","story":"The mango season has begun, and prices are swinging wildly at the market stalls. Srivalli needs your help to figure out exactly how much fruit she can afford as prices rise and fall — welcome to the world of demand.","teach":[{"title":"What is Demand?","body":"Demand is the quantity of a product people are willing AND able to buy at a particular price, depending on their needs, preferences, season, trend, and income. It is not just desire — it is desire backed by purchasing power.","visual":"🛍️"},{"title":"The Law of Demand","body":"When the price of a product rises, the quantity demanded decreases, and when the price falls, the quantity demanded increases. This inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded is called the Law of Demand.","visual":"📉"},{"title":"Srivalli's Demand Schedule","body":"At the start of mango season, mangoes cost ₹150/kg and Srivalli bought only 1 kg. As the price fell to ₹100, she bought 2 kg, and when it dropped to ₹50, she bought 3 kg. Plotting these points gives the downward-sloping individual demand curve DD'.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"What does the Law of Demand state?","options":["As price falls, quantity demanded rises (and vice versa)","As price falls, quantity demanded falls too","Demand always stays constant regardless of price","As price rises, quantity demanded also rises"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Law of Demand describes an inverse relationship between price and quantity demanded."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Demand is best defined as:","options":["The willingness AND ability (purchasing power) to buy a good at a price","Just the desire to own a good","The total goods produced by sellers","The price sellers want to charge"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter stresses demand is willingness complemented by the ability to pay."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At the start of mango season, Srivalli bought mangoes at ₹150/kg. How many kg did she buy?","answer":1,"explain":"At ₹150/kg, Srivalli's individual demand was 1 kg."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"When the price fell to ₹50 per kg, how many kg of mangoes did Srivalli buy?","answer":3,"explain":"At the lowest price of ₹50/kg, her demand rose to 3 kg."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"On the demand curve graph (Fig. 9.2), the demand curve DD' slopes:","options":["Downward from left to right","Upward from left to right","Horizontally, staying flat","Vertically, staying flat"],"answer":0,"explain":"A downward-sloping curve represents the inverse price-quantity relationship."}]},{"id":"pp2","title":"Market Demand","icon":"🛒","kind":"lesson","story":"Srivalli is not the only mango lover in town. Alex and Israt want mangoes too — add up their appetites and you'll see how the whole market's demand curve behaves differently from any one buyer's.","teach":[{"title":"From Individual to Market Demand","body":"Market demand is the total quantity of a good demanded by all potential buyers at different prices — it is the sum of all individual demand (Q1 + Q2 + Q3 = QD).","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Table 9.1 Worked Example","body":"At ₹150: Srivalli 1 kg + Alex 2 kg + Israt 3 kg = 6 kg market demand. At ₹100: 2 + 4 + 6 = 12 kg. At ₹50: 3 + 6 + 9 = 18 kg.","visual":"🥭"},{"title":"Why the Market Curve is Flatter","body":"The market demand curve is flatter than Srivalli's individual curve because it aggregates many consumers, so the same price change produces a larger total quantity response — when price fell from ₹150 to ₹50, Srivalli's demand rose by only 2 kg, but market demand rose by 12 kg.","visual":"📈"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At price ₹150, Srivalli demands 1 kg, Alex 2 kg, and Israt 3 kg. What is the total market demand (in kg)?","answer":6,"explain":"1 + 2 + 3 = 6 kg, as shown in Table 9.1."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At price ₹50, Srivalli demands 3 kg, Alex 6 kg, and Israt 9 kg. What is total market demand (in kg)?","answer":18,"explain":"3 + 6 + 9 = 18 kg, the highest market demand in Table 9.1."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Market demand is:","options":["The sum of the individual demand of all buyers in the market","The demand of only the richest buyer","The average price paid by all buyers","The total supply of a good"],"answer":0,"explain":"Market demand aggregates every individual buyer's demand at each price."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why is the market demand curve flatter than an individual's demand curve?","options":["Because many consumers together respond with a much larger change in quantity for the same price change","Because market prices never change","Because individuals buy more than the whole market","Because the market curve ignores price"],"answer":0,"explain":"Aggregating many buyers makes the total quantity response to a price change larger, flattening the curve."}]},{"id":"pp3","title":"Beyond Price: Other Demand Factors","icon":"🔄","kind":"lesson","story":"Prices are only half the story. Why do people rush to buy the newest smartphone even when it's expensive? Why do sweater sales spike in winter? Uncover the hidden forces that shift demand even when price stays the same.","teach":[{"title":"Related Goods: Substitutes","body":"Substitute goods can replace each other, like tea and coffee. If coffee's price rises while tea's stays the same, people may switch to tea, increasing its demand — so the demand for a good rises when the price of its substitute rises.","visual":"🔄"},{"title":"Related Goods: Complements","body":"Complementary goods are used together, like smartphones and earphones, or movie tickets and popcorn. If movie tickets become more expensive, people may go to the cinema less, so popcorn demand may fall too, even though popcorn's own price hasn't changed.","visual":"🔗"},{"title":"Income, Tastes & Population","body":"Rising income lets people afford more or better goods, raising demand. Personal tastes matter too — Srivalli prefers mangoes and won't switch to cheaper oranges. Population size and composition also shape demand: more children means more demand for sports shoes, more elderly means more demand for orthopaedic shoes.","visual":"👪"},{"title":"Diminishing Marginal Utility","body":"The additional usefulness (utility) gained from consuming more of a good declines with each extra unit — this is the diminishing marginal utility principle. As utility falls, willingness to pay falls too, so demand falls.","visual":"🍉"},{"title":"Seasonality & Future Expectations","body":"Demand shifts with seasons, festivals, and cultural habits — like sweaters in winter or crowded bookshops before a new school session. Future price expectations matter too: if people expect prices to rise, they buy now; if they expect a fall, they postpone purchases.","visual":"📅"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Tea and coffee are examples of:","options":["Substitute goods","Complementary goods","Public goods","Luxury goods only"],"answer":0,"explain":"Substitute goods can replace each other, like tea and coffee."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Smartphones and earphones are examples of:","options":["Complementary goods","Substitute goods","Identical goods","Unrelated goods"],"answer":0,"explain":"Complementary goods are used together to provide utility, like smartphones and earphones."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The principle that additional utility from consuming more of a good declines is called:","options":["Diminishing marginal utility","Law of demand","Law of supply","Market equilibrium"],"answer":0,"explain":"This principle explains why willingness to pay — and demand — falls as more of a good is consumed."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If consumers expect prices to rise in the future, what happens to present demand?","options":["It increases, as people buy immediately","It decreases, as people wait","It stays exactly the same","It becomes zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"Expecting higher future prices makes people buy now, raising present demand."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the price of coffee rises while tea's price stays the same, what happens to the demand for tea?","options":["It increases, as people switch to the substitute","It decreases","It has no effect at all","Tea disappears from the market"],"answer":0,"explain":"A rise in a substitute's price increases demand for the related good."}]},{"id":"pp4","title":"The Law of Supply","icon":"🏭","kind":"lesson","story":"Now switch sides of the stall. As a mango seller, higher prices mean fatter profits — and that changes exactly how much you're willing to bring to market.","teach":[{"title":"What is Supply?","body":"Supply is the quantity of a product that sellers are willing and able to offer at a particular price.","visual":"🏭"},{"title":"The Law of Supply","body":"As price increases, quantity supplied increases, and as price decreases, quantity supplied falls. This is the Law of Supply — higher prices increase profitability, incentivising producers to increase output and attracting new firms to the market.","visual":"📈"},{"title":"Seller A's Supply Schedule","body":"At ₹50/kg, Seller A supplies 1 kg; at ₹100/kg, 2 kg; at ₹150/kg, 3 kg. Plotting these points gives an upward-sloping individual supply curve.","visual":"📊"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"The Law of Supply states that:","options":["As price rises, quantity supplied increases (and vice versa)","As price rises, quantity supplied decreases","Supply never changes with price","Higher prices always lower profits"],"answer":0,"explain":"Supply and price move in the same direction — a direct relationship."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"According to Seller A's supply schedule, how many kg of mangoes does the seller supply at ₹150 per kg?","answer":3,"explain":"At the highest price, ₹150/kg, Seller A supplies 3 kg."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At ₹50 per kg, how many kg does Seller A supply?","answer":1,"explain":"At the lowest price shown, ₹50/kg, Seller A supplies only 1 kg."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"On a supply curve graph, the supply curve slopes:","options":["Upward from left to right","Downward from left to right","Flat/horizontal","It cannot be graphed"],"answer":0,"explain":"The supply curve is upward-sloping since higher prices lead to greater quantity supplied."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does a higher price incentivise producers to supply more?","options":["It increases profitability and attracts new firms to the market","It makes production automatically cheaper","It reduces consumer demand to zero","It has no effect on producer behaviour"],"answer":0,"explain":"Higher prices raise profitability, encouraging existing and new producers to supply more."}]},{"id":"pp5","title":"Beyond Price: Other Supply Factors","icon":"⚙️","kind":"lesson","story":"Three sellers, A, B, and C, have set up shop in the mango market. Combine their offerings, then discover what makes farmers switch crops, technology boost harvests, and wholesalers hold back stock.","teach":[{"title":"Market Supply","body":"Market supply is the sum of all individual sellers' supply (A + B + C = QS). From Table 9.2: at ₹50, sellers A, B, C supply 1 + 3 + 2 = 6 kg; at ₹150, they supply 3 + 7 + 8 = 18 kg.","visual":"➕"},{"title":"Price of Related Goods (A Producer's Choice)","body":"Suppose a farmer can grow wheat or chickpeas. If chickpea prices are high and wheat prices are low, the farmer will grow more chickpeas next season, since supply depends on the relative profitability of alternatives.","visual":"🌾"},{"title":"Number of Sellers & Technology","body":"More sellers in a market raises supply, which can push it above demand and lower prices; fewer sellers lowers supply and can raise prices. Better technology, like drip irrigation, weather sensors, or cold storage, reduces production costs and increases supply.","visual":"🔧"},{"title":"Future Expectations","body":"If producers expect demand to boom, they produce and supply more now. If they expect prices to rise later, they may hold back supply now to sell later at higher prices — as potato wholesalers sometimes do before the peak season.","visual":"🔮"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At price ₹50, Seller A supplies 1 kg, Seller B 3 kg, Seller C 2 kg. What is the total market supply (in kg)?","answer":6,"explain":"1 + 3 + 2 = 6 kg, as shown in Table 9.2."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"At price ₹150, Sellers A, B and C supply 3, 7, and 8 kg respectively. What is total market supply (in kg)?","answer":18,"explain":"3 + 7 + 8 = 18 kg, the highest market supply in Table 9.2."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If chickpea prices are high and wheat prices are low, what will a rational farmer likely do next season?","options":["Grow more chickpeas, since they are more profitable","Grow more wheat regardless of profit","Stop farming completely","Grow equal amounts of both crops always"],"answer":0,"explain":"Supply decisions depend on the relative profitability of alternative goods a producer could offer."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"How does improved technology (like drip irrigation) typically affect supply?","options":["It reduces production costs and increases supply","It always reduces supply","It has no effect on production","It only affects demand, not supply"],"answer":0,"explain":"Lower production costs from better technology let producers supply more."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If potato wholesalers expect prices to rise later, what might they do with supply now?","options":["Hold back supply now to sell later at higher prices","Sell everything immediately at any price","Destroy their stock","Give away potatoes for free"],"answer":0,"explain":"Future price expectations can make suppliers withhold goods now to profit later."}]},{"id":"pp6","title":"Market Equilibrium","icon":"⚖️","kind":"lesson","story":"Demand and supply are pulling against each other like two teams in a tug-of-war. Find the exact price where neither side is pulling — that magic point is market equilibrium.","teach":[{"title":"What is Market Equilibrium?","body":"Market equilibrium is the point where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied. At this point, there is no pressure for prices to change — the market is 'cleared', with neither a shortage (excess demand) nor a surplus (excess supply).","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Table 9.3 Worked Example","body":"At ₹40: Qd = 38 kg, Qs = 6 kg — excess demand. At ₹100: Qd = 12 kg, Qs = 12 kg — market equilibrium (equilibrium price = ₹100, equilibrium quantity = 12 kg). At ₹150: Qd = 8 kg, Qs = 43 kg — excess supply.","visual":"📋"},{"title":"Does Equilibrium Exist in the Real World?","body":"In theory, equilibrium is a fixed intersection point, but real markets are dynamic — technology, wages, interest rates, wars, pandemics, and weather constantly shift demand and supply, so the market is always adjusting toward a new equilibrium rather than settling permanently.","visual":"🌍"},{"title":"Dynamic Pricing in Action","body":"During COVID-19, demand for face masks surged, supply couldn't catch up immediately, and prices rose sharply — then fell as suppliers adjusted, and fell further once the pandemic ended. Similarly, a Goa hotel charges ₹1,500/night off-season, ₹8,000 on a peak-season weekend, and ₹25,000 on New Year's Eve.","visual":"🏨"}],"questions":[{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the chapter's mango market table, at price ₹100 both quantity demanded and quantity supplied equal how many kg (the equilibrium quantity)?","answer":12,"explain":"Table 9.3 shows equilibrium quantity = 12 kg at the equilibrium price of ₹100."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At price ₹40 in Table 9.3, Qd = 38 kg and Qs = 6 kg. This situation is called:","options":["Excess demand (a shortage)","Excess supply (a surplus)","Market equilibrium","A price floor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, creating excess demand."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"At price ₹150 in Table 9.3, Qd = 8 kg and Qs = 43 kg. This situation is called:","options":["Excess supply (a surplus)","Excess demand (a shortage)","Market equilibrium","A price floor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Quantity supplied exceeds quantity demanded, creating excess supply."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the equilibrium price of mangoes in Table 9.3 (in ₹)?","answer":100,"explain":"At ₹100, quantity demanded equals quantity supplied at 12 kg."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"During the COVID-19 pandemic, what happened to face mask prices when demand surged but supply couldn't catch up immediately?","options":["Prices rose significantly, then fell as suppliers adjusted","Prices fell immediately","Prices stayed exactly the same","Masks were given away for free"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter describes mask prices rising sharply, then falling as supply caught up and demand later dropped."}]},{"id":"pp7","title":"The Government's Balancing Act","icon":"🏛️","kind":"lesson","story":"Markets don't always play fair — sometimes essential medicines get too expensive, or a single seller takes over completely. Step into the government's shoes and learn when to step in, and when to step back.","teach":[{"title":"Why Government Intervenes","body":"Markets allocate goods and services based on willingness and ability to pay, which isn't always fair — for instance, if essential medicines become very expensive, they may not be accessible to all. The government intervenes to protect fairness and the welfare of vulnerable groups.","visual":"🏛️"},{"title":"Price Ceiling & Price Floor","body":"A price ceiling is an imposed maximum price a seller can charge — like the government capping essential medicine prices. A price floor is an imposed minimum price, such as a minimum wage; for a price floor to be effective, it must be set above the market equilibrium price.","visual":"🚧"},{"title":"Regulating Monopoly & Market Regulators","body":"A monopoly is a market structure with a single seller controlling the entire supply of a good with no close substitutes, giving it power to charge higher prices and restrict supply — harming consumer welfare. Regulators like the RBI, Central Consumer Protection Authority, TRAI, and SEBI help ensure fairness and transparency.","visual":"🔍"},{"title":"Public Goods","body":"Public goods like roads, streetlights, parks, and national defence are provided by the government because they benefit everyone but don't generate direct profit for private companies — if a neighbourhood park needs ₹5,000 per family, many people assume others will pay, so it never gets built without government funding.","visual":"🛣️"},{"title":"Limitations of Government Intervention","body":"Excessive intervention can backfire: fixing wheat's price at ₹20/kg when the market price is ₹30/kg gives farmers less than market value, reducing their incentive to produce. Heavy regulation also creates compliance burdens for small businesses and discourages innovation, since producers won't invest in better technology if they can't earn adequate returns.","visual":"⚠️"}],"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A price ceiling is:","options":["An imposed maximum price a seller can charge","An imposed minimum price a seller must charge","The equilibrium price only","A tax on luxury goods"],"answer":0,"explain":"A price ceiling caps the highest price that can legally be charged."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A price floor is:","options":["An imposed minimum price, which must be set above equilibrium to be effective","An imposed maximum price","The same as a price ceiling","Only used for essential medicines"],"answer":0,"explain":"A price floor sets a lower limit, like a minimum wage, and must sit above equilibrium to have any effect."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"During COVID-19, the government capped the maximum retail price of a 200 ml sanitiser bottle under the Essential Commodities Act, 1955, at how many rupees?","answer":100,"explain":"The chapter states the price ceiling for 200 ml sanitiser bottles was ₹100."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A monopoly is a market structure where:","options":["A single seller controls the entire supply with no close substitutes, gaining power to set prices and output","Many sellers compete on price","The government sets all prices","Buyers control the supply of goods"],"answer":0,"explain":"A monopoly means one seller dominates, which can be detrimental to consumer welfare."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"If the government fixes wheat's price at ₹20/kg while the market price is ₹30/kg, what is a likely effect?","options":["Farmers receive less than market value, reducing incentive to produce, possibly causing shortages","Farmers will produce even more wheat than before","There will be no effect on farmers","Wheat prices will rise further"],"answer":0,"explain":"Price distortions below market levels reduce producer incentives and can cause shortages."}]},{"id":"pp-boss","title":"The Equilibrium Trial","icon":"🏆","kind":"boss","story":"The marketplace itself rises against you as a living storm of shifting prices — demand surging, supply wavering, and the government watching from the sidelines. To close out Part 1 of your journey, read every signal at once and find the single point where the chaos settles: equilibrium.","teach":[{"title":"Demand & Supply Recap","body":"The Law of Demand shows an inverse relationship — as price falls, quantity demanded rises. The Law of Supply shows a direct relationship — as price rises, quantity supplied rises. Both are shaped by more than price alone: income, related goods, tastes, population, technology, and future expectations all matter.","visual":"⚖️"},{"title":"Equilibrium Recap","body":"Market equilibrium occurs where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied — in the chapter's mango example, that's ₹100 for 12 kg. Real markets never fully settle, constantly adjusting toward new equilibria as weather, trends, technology, and income shift.","visual":"🎯"},{"title":"Government's Balancing Act Recap","body":"Governments use price ceilings, price floors, regulation of monopolies, and provision of public goods to protect fairness and welfare — but excessive intervention can distort prices, burden businesses with compliance, and discourage innovation.","visual":"🏛️"}],"boss":{"name":"The Price Tempest","taunt":"Every price you fix, I will unfix — find equilibrium before the market swallows you whole!"},"questions":[{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Law of Demand, when the price of a good rises, the quantity demanded:","options":["Falls","Rises","Stays exactly the same","Becomes infinite"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the inverse price-quantity relationship of the Law of Demand."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"According to the Law of Supply, when the price of a good rises, the quantity supplied:","options":["Rises, since higher prices increase profitability","Falls","Stays the same","Drops to zero"],"answer":0,"explain":"This is the direct price-quantity relationship of the Law of Supply."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"In the chapter's mango market equilibrium table, what is the equilibrium quantity (in kg)?","answer":12,"explain":"At the equilibrium price of ₹100, both quantity demanded and supplied equal 12 kg."},{"kind":"numeric","prompt":"What is the equilibrium price (in ₹) in that same mango market table?","answer":100,"explain":"₹100 is the price where quantity demanded equals quantity supplied."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"When price is below equilibrium and quantity demanded exceeds quantity supplied, this is called:","options":["Excess demand (a shortage)","Excess supply (a surplus)","Market equilibrium","A price floor"],"answer":0,"explain":"Quantity demanded exceeding quantity supplied at a given price is excess demand."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Movie tickets and popcorn sold in cinema halls are an example of:","options":["Complementary goods","Substitute goods","Public goods","Unrelated goods"],"answer":0,"explain":"They are typically consumed together, so a rise in ticket prices can reduce popcorn demand too."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"A price ceiling on an essential medicine is set as:","options":["A maximum price sellers may charge, at or below the market price","A minimum price sellers must charge, above the market price","Exactly equal to production cost always","A price that only applies during festivals"],"answer":0,"explain":"A price ceiling caps the maximum a seller can legally charge."},{"kind":"mcq","prompt":"Why does the chapter argue that markets are 'dynamic systems' rather than machines with fixed equilibria?","options":["Because conditions like technology, weather, income, and events constantly shift demand and supply, moving equilibrium","Because prices never actually change in real markets","Because government always fixes every price","Because equilibrium is reached once and never changes again"],"answer":0,"explain":"The chapter closes by describing markets as constantly adapting and evolving, never settling permanently."}]}]}];